version: "2.0"
description: >-
  The Warpline Engine. Phase 1: the Convergence/Divergence Oracle — a read-only
  tool that lifts two git branches to a content-addressed store of MEANING (the
  Paradigm symbol graph), predicts the merge from meaning (clean / knot /
  dangling), runs git's actual merge read-only, and scores where meaning and
  bytes agreed or diverged. Phase 2 (the native WRITE PATH): #fabric/#pick seal
  the working meaning into this project's OWN durable history (#strand records in
  .warpline/fabric.jsonl + the .warpline/refs/selvage tip), single-writer, NO git
  merge — git coexists (TD-2026-06-25-564). Multi-writer SCRATCH+CAS+WEFT is
  deferred (Phase C, gated on the false-AUTOFOLD safety experiment). Warpline only
  ever writes under .warpline/; the user's git HEAD/index/worktree are never touched.

components:
  git-exec:
    description: >-
      Thin, READ-ONLY wrappers over git (merge-base, rev-parse, commit meta,
      merge-tree). ABSORB materializes a ref's tree via `git archive | tar` into a
      temp dir — NO worktree, no .git/worktrees lock — so absorbs run concurrently
      (T-2026-06-23-003). worktreeAdd/Remove remain only for the cold git<2.38
      merge-tree fallback, which needs real merge machinery and is serialized
      per-repo via #repo-lock. gitUserName reads `git config user.name` (read-only)
      to seed the default ACTOR of a fabric #pick — coexistence only, never written
      back. The user's HEAD/index/worktree are never touched. PERF PLUMBING
      (T-2026-07-04-003): catFileBatch streams many blobs through ONE `git cat-file
      --batch` process (fail-closed on any missing object — a partial snapshot must
      never look complete; previously one `git show` spawn per file made whole-tree
      snapshots O(minutes) on a real monorepo), and diffRaw returns full-sha
      (--no-abbrev — raw-format abbreviation is NOT governed by --full-index) per-path
      change records (--raw -z --no-renames) — the changed-path inventory the
      INCREMENTAL native snapshot overlays onto a parent tree.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#repo-lock"

  repo-lock:
    description: >-
      A per-repo, in-process async mutex (withRepoLock(repoRoot, fn)). Serializes
      the one remaining git-worktree user — the cold git<2.38 merge-tree fallback —
      per resolved repo root so concurrent calls can't race the .git/worktrees lock;
      distinct repos run free. A predecessor's rejection never blocks later callers.
      In-process only (cross-process would need a .git flock; the parallel-absorb
      path no longer needs a worktree at all).
    type: module

  canonical-json:
    description: >-
      Strict, dependency-free canonical serializer (sorted keys, NFC strings, no
      whitespace ambiguity) that produces the byte stream the essence hash digests.
    type: module

  essence-hash:
    description: >-
      The load-bearing wall — essence(symbol) -> contentId. Hashes the Canonical
      Normal Form ⟨kind, normalized contract, edgeBag⟩, Merkle by TARGET essence
      (so rename is free), excludes name/path/prose/timestamps. Tarjan SCC for
      cycles; stable-id fold for generic-contract aliasing. .purpose contentId =
      "essence:v0:" + sha256(canonical). STAGE 3a: a code-unit branch in localCNF
      (detected by componentType==='code-unit') replaces the sorted edgeBag with
      the CCNF body and SUBSTITUTES each positional f:N token INLINE with the
      essence of codeLocalTargets[N]'s edge (via the SAME edgeEssence resolver, so
      intra-SCC targets become @scc-internal and a cross-symbol rename frees the
      chain); extern/builtin free:name tokens stay untouched; edgeBag is []. The
      version tag is read off entry.data.essenceTag (default ESSENCE_VERSION) so a
      code-unit contentId is "essence:<algo>:ts<exact>:"+sha256(canonical) (algo
      currently v1.1) and SCC members carry the same tag — mixed v0/v1.1 graphs
      interoperate because Merkle substitutes the target's full TAGGED string.
      essence-hash never imports the lens; the tag travels on the node.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#canonical-json"
      - "#warp-object"

  warp-object:
    description: >-
      A WarpObject — a SymbolEntry stripped of labels and reduced to ⟨kind,
      contract, sorted outgoing edges, contentId⟩. liftEdges derives outgoing,
      typed, deduped, sorted edges from the live SymbolIndex (the single edge
      source the essence hash agrees on).
    type: module

  warp-state:
    description: >-
      A WarpState — the whole symbol graph of one ref lifted to MEANING. stateId =
      sha256 over the sorted contentIds; treeSha/absorbedAt are provenance, not
      identity. WarpObject.filePath is normalized REPO-RELATIVE to the absorb root
      (temp-worktree or cwd, with macOS /private↔/var alias handling) so a
      path-prefix difference never masquerades as a rename.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#warp-object"
      - "#essence-hash"

  warp-store:
    description: >-
      Content-addressed store for WARP objects + states and the .warpline/oracle.jsonl
      ledger appender. In-mem primary with a .warpline/ disk cache; loadState(stateId)
      rehydrates a persisted state back from .warpline/states/ (the Phase-2 write path
      needs the PARENT state across CLI runs when the in-mem map is cold) — faithful
      because diff reads only stableKey/contentId/contract, all preserved by
      serializeState. The ONLY thing Warpline writes to disk is under .warpline/.
      M1a — the NATIVE OBJECT STORE (byte authority, the replacement build): blob.ts
      (raw-byte blob, blobId=sha256 over git-style "blob <len>\0<bytes>" framing +
      shadow gitBlobOid), tree.ts (sorted directory manifest, git TREE-ORDER, fixed-
      width binary ids parseable by mode, treeId=sha256 + shadow gitTreeOid), object-
      store.ts (loose zlib blobs/ + trees/ under .warpline/objects/ with git fan-out,
      write-tmp+rename, idempotent put, verify()), snapshot.ts (snapshotDir walks a
      tree writing every object → root treeId + shadow gitOid; restoreTree rebuilds a
      byte-faithful tree with git ABSENT — binary/exec/symlink/empty-dir parity, and
      returns the entry count). IGNORE SEMANTICS (T-031/HIGH-3, the dogfooding
      unblock): the WORKTREE walk (snapshotDir, behind worktree pick/admit) honors
      ignore-rules.ts — .warplineignore at the root when present (gitignore syntax
      via the `ignore` package: dir patterns, globs, negation !, anchored /), else
      .gitignore, plus ALWAYS-ignored .git/.warpline/.loom/node_modules at any depth
      (.loom = the Loom-era engine's own state dir, tool state never meaning —
      T-2026-07-17-007) —
      so dependency trees and secrets never enter the permanent no-gc store;
      ignored dirs are PRUNED (no re-inclusion inside an excluded dir, gitignore
      parity). THE TREE SEMANTICS DECISION (T-2026-07-18-005; fixes the F3 drill-#1
      recover false-refusal T-2026-07-18-004): WORKTREE SEMANTICS ('worktree:v1' —
      the snapshotDir rules above) is THE canonical tree semantics for ALL NEW
      bindings; the REF path (snapshotRef, full AND incremental) filters by the
      ref's OWN committed root ignore rules, so ref tree ≡ worktree tree of the
      same clean checkout (tracked-but-gitignored files OUT — git's view, not the
      walk's). New bindings carry additive binding.treeSemantics:'worktree:v1'
      (OUTSIDE the pickId preimage in both epochs; the founder-signed v3 preimage
      untouched); ABSENT = legacy-git semantics, grandfathered — every strand is
      judged under ITS OWN recorded semantics. projectTreeWorktreeSemantics(store,
      treeId) = the pure deterministic projection of any store tree to its
      worktree-semantics view (filtered by the tree's own root ignore blobs; zero
      writes) — how #stake cut/recover derive a legacy binding's honest recovery
      expectation. A root ignore-rule change between anchor and ref falls OPEN to
      the full walk. Pinned by test/tree-semantics.test.ts + snapshot-ignore.test.ts.
      TRUST FLOOR (MEDIUM-1): getBlob/getTree are VERIFIED
      READS — always-on, they recompute the content-address of the raw framed bytes
      just inflated and FAIL CLOSED on a mismatch, so restore/materialize/verify can
      never consume a tampered loose object (one uniform rule, no trusted/untrusted
      read fork). T-033 (root-ignore symmetry): snapshotRef skips .git/.warpline
      path components at ANY depth — a repo that TRACKS its own fabric ledger (this
      one does) would otherwise bind a ref tree that never matches the worktree
      snapshot and that restoreTree refuses to write. M1c PATH-HARDENING (Aegis C3,
      CVE-2021-21300 class):
      restoreTree FAILS CLOSED on a forged/corrupt entry name (assertSafeEntryName
      rejects empty/./../slash/backslash/NUL/absolute/drive-letter + reserved
      .git/.warpline), asserts the parent dir is a real non-symlink directory, and
      never writes THROUGH a pre-existing symlink — the #restore verb consumes it. The
      shadow gitOid EQUALS `git rev-parse <ref>^{tree}` for a clean worktree (a free
      byte-faithfulness proof during coexistence). Names use RAW bytes not NFC (A5).
      CLI: `warpline objects snapshot <dir>` / `objects verify`. Sha256 (one hash
      across meaning + bytes) — spec packages/warpline/docs/native-object-store-design.md.
      DELTA-NATIVE SNAPSHOTS (T-2026-07-04-003 byte layer, the Move-3 full-run
      unblock): snapshotRef has two byte-IDENTICAL paths — FULL (ls-tree inventory +
      ONE catFileBatch stream; was one git-show spawn per file, ~30s on this
      monorepo, now ~2s) and INCREMENTAL (a verified SnapshotAnchor {ref, treeId} →
      `git diff --raw` overlay onto the anchor tree via writeMergedTree, so an admit
      snapshot costs the DIFF, not the universe; sub-second warm). The incremental
      path applies the T-033 .git/.warpline skip identically and falls OPEN to the
      full walk on any anomaly (never fail-closed on the anchor). strandSnapshotAnchor
      is the verification gate: only a non-merge strand tagged
      binding.treeSemantics:'worktree:v1' whose binding.gitOid still
      equals `rev-parse <provenance.gitCommit>^{tree}` and whose native tree is
      present anchors — a merge strand's gitCommit is one parent, worktree/backfilled
      seals carry gitOid null, LEGACY-semantics (untagged) bindings must never seed a
      worktree-semantics overlay: all rejected (the first post-cutover seal walks
      full once, then re-anchors). captureMerge anchors base on the base
      strand's verified binding and ours/theirs on the just-built base tree (three
      universe snapshots → three diffs). Equivalence pinned by
      test/snapshot-incremental.test.ts (full treeId === incremental treeId on real
      fixtures incl. tracked-.warpline, mode flips, symlinks, deletes; plus a
      path-taken proof so fail-open can't mask a broken incremental path).
      I5 — THE WORKTREE INDEX (NATIVE-FIRST phase 0, T-2026-07-17-008): the
      WORKTREE walk gets the same delta discipline via worktree-index.ts, a git-
      style stat cache (`.warpline/index`, worktreeIndex:v1, atomic tmp+rename,
      per-worktree sections of [mtimeMs,size,ino,mode,blobId,gitSha] tuples).
      snapshotDir(store, dir, {indexRoot}) trusts an entry only when the full
      stat key matches (mode included — a bare chmod is a miss), the recorded
      mtime clears the RACY_WINDOW_MS guard against builtAt (git's racy-
      timestamp lesson), and the cached blobId is still present in the store
      (a doctored index can never invent bytes); every anomaly fails OPEN to
      the full read+hash walk, and the refreshed index is written back after
      every walk. Warm walk on this monorepo (23k files): 7.1s full → 0.7-1.6s
      indexed, byte-identical treeId+gitOid (pinned with mutation-detection
      touch/edit/add/delete/chmod + path-taken + racy-guard + fail-open tests
      in test/worktree-index.test.ts). Wired behind native propose/resolve,
      worktree pick/admit (snapshotState indexRoot), and `objects snapshot`.
    type: module

  code-lens:
    description: >-
      The TS code-lens interface surface (spec §2 / warpline-code-lens.md). Types
      ONLY (stage 1): CodeUnit — a SymbolEntry-shaped synthetic node keyed
      #code:<rel-path>::<qualified-name>, carrying the identity-bearing
      codeEssence (CCNF) body slot, resolved free-reference edges, and a visible
      reducedFidelity marker — and the CodeLens registry contract (extensions +
      lift()). Declares no behavior; pins the shape the lift machinery (ts-lens,
      stage 2) is built against. componentType 'code-unit' lets code-units flow
      through the EXISTING WARP (essence-hash/sem-delta/predict/oracle) as nodes
      in the same universe, never a parallel graph.
    type: module

  code-essence:
    description: >-
      The pure-syntactic Code Canonical Normal Form (CCNF) producer — codeCNF(node)
      -> canonical token string for one declaration. The SYNTACTIC HALF of the
      lens: structure + WRITTEN annotations only, NO ts.Program/checker. Hardens
      the AST-absorb spike: (1) scope-correct alpha-normalization honoring JS
      hoisting (var/fn-decl -> nearest function scope; let/const/class -> block;
      params/type-params -> unit scope; catch var -> catch scope) with bindings as
      (depth, ordinal) coordinates and shadowing-correct use resolution, replacing
      the spike's unsound flat first-declaration-order map; (2) separate b:/t:/L:
      namespaces (the spike ignored type-params, so f<T> vs f<U> falsely DIFFERed);
      (3) the false-EQUAL guard (§3.2), FAIL-CLOSED since CCNF v1.1 (amendment
      2026-07-02): EVERY modifier present is serialized (canonical token name,
      sorted — accessibility/override/readonly/accessor/declare/async/static/
      abstract/export and any future modifier), decorators are serialized on
      function-likes AND parameters in SOURCE order (order is semantic) with
      their expressions through the standard expression path so decorator refs
      join the §4 frontier, type-param modifiers (<const T>) included; plus
      generator*/get-set/optional?/default-presence/rest.../definite!/
      optional-chain?./nullish?? each produce a DISTINCT cnf. Exports
      CCNF_ALGO_VERSION ('v1.1') — bumped on ANY serialization change so
      cross-version essence comparison is impossible;
      test/false-equal-probes.test.ts is the per-TS-release ship gate;
      (4) literal normalization (§3.3) — integers
      (hex/oct/bin) -> exact decimal, bigint -> decimal+n, non-integer numerics ->
      raw source lexeme NOT an IEEE-754 round-trip (0.1 != 1e-1 by design),
      strings/templates NFC; (5) directive comments (@ts-ignore/@ts-expect-error/
      @ts-nocheck/eslint-disable) hashed, free prose dropped. An optional
      freeRefClassifier strips free names to positional f:{idx} edge tokens (default
      emits free:{name}, keeping stage 1 self-contained and spike-equivalent).
      Stage 2 adds codeCNFDetailed(node,opts) -> {cnf, freeRefs[]}: the SAME
      first-appearance ordering drives both the body's f:{idx} tokens and the
      freeRefs index, so freeRefs[idx] (name + a representative ts.Identifier node)
      aligns 1:1 with the f:idx token. codeCNF now delegates to it and returns .cnf
      (the stage-1 signature is unchanged). This positional alignment is the input
      the lens hands the checker for §4 resolution; call order is meaning, so a
      sorted edge-set would be wrong.
    type: module

  code-symbol:
    description: >-
      Pure string builders for a code-unit's WARP key and structural stable key
      (spec §2). codeSymbol(relPath, qualifiedName) -> "#code:"+relPath+"::"+
      qualifiedName (the SymbolIndex key); codeStableKey(relPath, structuralPath)
      -> relPath+"::"+structuralPath where structuralPath is the (scopeKind#ordinal)
      chain (e.g. class#0/method#2). Both are LABELS — provenance + rename
      tiebreakers — NEVER hashed; pure functions of their inputs (no I/O), so
      trivially deterministic across machines.
    type: module

  ts-lens:
    description: >-
      The TS/TSX code-lens (stage 2, spec §4/§5) — the checker-dependent half.
      TsLens.lift(rootDir) enumerates .ts/.tsx files (sorted, .d.ts excluded),
      builds ONE ts.Program over them against a FIXED, HARDCODED compiler-options
      baseline (ESNext target/module, Bundler resolution, strict, skipLibCheck,
      noEmit, allowJs:false — NEVER a discovered tsconfig, §5.2), and lifts each
      code-unit (FunctionDeclaration, arrow/fn-expr const, class + its method/ctor/
      getter/setter/field members; default-export -> qualifiedName 'default';
      overload sets -> the implementation signature, bodiless overloads skipped).
      Per unit it computes codeEssence via codeCNFDetailed and resolves each free
      VALUE ref to one of four §4 classes, ALIGNED to the body's f:idx slots: (a)
      module-local lifted code-unit -> 'local' edge (edgeKind calls/reads) carrying
      the target #code: symbol (body emits f:idx); (b) imported name -> 'extern' id
      extern:<specifier-as-written>#<exportName> derived from the SYNTACTIC import
      statement, NOT checker module resolution (§5.3 — deterministic with no
      node_modules); (c) builtin/global -> 'builtin' name; (d) unresolved -> set
      reducedFidelity. Checker is used for identity resolution ONLY (getSymbolAtLocation
      + getAliasedSymbol), never inferred types (§5.1). lift() returns CodeUnit[]
      sorted by symbol (determinism). Exports TS_LENS_VERSION (the exact pinned ts
      version) for stage 3 to stamp into the essence tag.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#code-essence"
      - "#code-symbol"
      - "#code-lens"

  lens-registry:
    description: >-
      The code-lens registry (spec §9). lensFor(ext) -> CodeLens | undefined maps a
      file extension (incl. leading dot) to the responsible lens. Registered: TsLens
      (.ts/.tsx) and CfgLens (.json/.yml/.yaml — #cfg-lens, P3 GAP-1). The single
      seam per-language lenses plug into without touching the lift/absorb path.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#code-lens"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#cfg-lens"

  cfg-lens:
    description: >-
      The structured-data lens (P3 Lane A, GAP-1) — JSON/YAML key-trees lifted to
      CodeUnit-shaped nodes so config files (the census-proven agent-collision
      center of mass: package.json, tsconfig, CI yaml) get MEANING verdicts instead
      of falling silently to the byte tier. GRANULARITY (cfg-v1): one file-root
      marker unit per parsed file (key '/'); a top-level key whose value is a
      non-empty map lifts ONE UNIT PER CHILD (/dependencies/react) so disjoint dep
      adds COMMUTE (autoClean) while the same dep at different versions is a
      same-unit divergent body → KNOT via predict's scalar 'body' slot rule; a
      top-level scalar key is one unit; a child's whole value subtree is one leaf.
      SEQUENCES defer to the byte tier: any unit whose subtree contains an array
      becomes a content-INDEPENDENT reduced-fidelity 'seq' marker (order is meaning
      the key-tree algebra cannot honestly claim — pretending would knot CI-step
      edits the token merge composes today). DETERMINISM: raw scalar LEXEMES
      (as-written is meaning, mirrors ts-essence's literal discipline; 1.0 ≠ 1);
      map key ORDER is not meaning (canonical sort — a pure reorder is the empty
      delta); the KEY PATH and FILE PATH are folded into the body (documented
      exception to labels-never-hashed: for structured data the key path IS the
      contract and the file location IS meaning; cost: cfg file rename reads as
      retire+born). Essence tag cfg-v1 (own content-address namespace, never
      colliding with TS CCNF). FAIL-CLOSED: unparseable / duplicate-key /
      non-map-root / multi-document / anchor-alias-tag/merge-key YAML / oversize
      (>1MiB) files fall to the byte tier with a single VISIBLE reduced-fidelity
      'unliftable' marker unit — never silent-empty, never a false verdict. JSON is
      parsed by a strict local scanner with JSONC comforts (comments + trailing
      commas — tsconfig reality; comments are prose, never hashed) and raw-lexeme +
      duplicate-key fidelity JSON.parse cannot provide; YAML via the `yaml` package,
      plain core schema only. Lockfiles are excluded entirely (#derived-artifacts).
      Skips node_modules/.git/dist/build/.next/coverage plus .warpline (the fabric
      never lifts itself), .loom (the Loom-era engine's state dir — same rule,
      prior name; T-2026-07-17-007: worktree absorbs were lifting thousands of
      #cfg:.loom/states/* symbols into every verdict) and .paradigm
      (machine-managed state).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#code-lens"
      - "#code-symbol"
      - "#canonical-json"
      - "#derived-artifacts"

  derived-artifacts:
    description: >-
      The DERIVED-ARTIFACT rule (P3 GAP-1). Lockfiles (package-lock.json,
      npm-shrinkwrap.json, yarn.lock, pnpm-lock.yaml, bun.lockb/.lock, deno.lock,
      Cargo.lock, composer.lock, Gemfile.lock, poetry.lock, Pipfile.lock,
      flake.lock) are machine-generated projections of the manifests beside them —
      derived, never meaning. isDerivedArtifact(relPath) matches by explicit
      BASENAME set (no glob — fail-open here would silently wrong-merge a file we
      pretended to understand). Consumers: #cfg-lens never lifts them; #materialize
      never knots them (both-sides-divergent → take OURS wholesale + a STALE marker
      in MergePlan.derivedStale — regeneration from the merged manifests is the
      consumer's step, recorded not performed in v1); #honesty labels them
      'derived'.
    type: module

  honesty:
    description: >-
      Per-path merge HONESTY labels (P3 GAP-1) — "what fraction of this merge did
      meaning govern?" classifyMergePaths(paths, states) labels every touched path
      exactly one of: meaning-decided (a lens lifted REAL units from the file — TS
      code-units, cfg key units, .purpose symbols; cfg MARKER units never claim
      coverage), derived (#derived-artifacts), or byte-decided (everything else —
      README, unparseable configs, sequence-valued keys). Returns sorted perPath +
      aggregate counts {meaningDecided, byteDecided, derived}. G1-ADDITIVE
      presentation data, never a verdict input: attached as AdmitResult.coverage
      (on a materialized CLEAN admit, classified against proposed/selvage/merged
      states) and OracleRecord.coverage (over changedPaths(base,A) ∪ (base,B),
      real refs only; schemaVersion unchanged).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#derived-artifacts"

  lift-code-units:
    description: >-
      The absorb-side bridge (stage 3a, spec §9 sub-phase 2 / §4.1).
      liftCodeUnits(rootDir) runs every REGISTERED lens over a read-only worktree
      root and collects the union of CodeUnits (re-sorted by symbol for
      deterministic injection order). injectCodeUnits(index, units) turns each
      CodeUnit into a synthetic SymbolEntry (kind 'component' + componentType
      'code-unit'; DETERMINISTIC id = sha256('code:'+symbol), NOT a uuid; source
      'premise'; references = the distinct LOCAL targets in first-appearance order
      so liftEdges yields code->localTarget edges into buildEssenceGraph.adj and
      Tarjan detects code cycles) and splices it into the live SymbolIndex
      (entries + byType + bySource) BEFORE computeEssences runs — same universe,
      never a parallel graph. The identity-bearing payload travels on entry.data:
      codeEssence (body with positional f:idx slots), codeLocalTargets (the
      ordered distinct local target symbols the f:N slots substitute against), and
      essenceTag = CCNF_ALGO_VERSION+":ts"+TS_LENS_VERSION (e.g. "v1.1:ts5.9.3" —
      the algorithm- and compiler-pinned version namespace §5.2, so essence-hash
      never imports the lens — the tag travels with the node). P3 GAP-1: a unit may
      OVERRIDE the tag per-unit (CodeUnit.essenceTag — #cfg-lens stamps cfg-v1) and
      carry a cfgMarker discriminator into entry.data for the #honesty classifier.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#code-lens"
      - "#lens-registry"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#cfg-lens"

  absorb:
    description: >-
      absorb(gitRef) -> WarpState. Materializes the ref's tree via `git archive |
      tar` into a temp dir (NO worktree, no .git/worktrees lock — concurrency-safe,
      T-2026-06-23-003), loadLiveGraph on it, lifts every symbol, builds the
      WarpState, releaseTree in a finally. The literal WORKTREE ref absorbs the live
      cwd uncommitted state.
      STAGE 3a: after loadLiveGraph and before buildWarpState (both the ref and
      WORKTREE paths), liftCodeUnits + injectCodeUnits splice TS/TSX code-units
      into the index as synthetic nodes so code essences flow through the same
      WARP. The lens only READS the worktree/cwd — the read-only invariant holds.
      DETERMINISM INVARIANT: absorbing the same ref twice is byte-identical
      (stateId + every per-symbol contentId + repo-relative filePath), so
      diff(absorb(HEAD), absorb(HEAD)) is provably ZERO. Rests on (a) the absorb
      root being passed to buildWarpState for repo-relative filePaths, (b)
      deterministic .purpose/portal file-discovery order in premise-core's
      aggregator, and (c) the lens's sorted file enumeration + deterministic
      synthetic ids (duplicate-symbol resolution + Merkle essences are stable
      across parses regardless of filesystem order or temp-dir prefix).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#git-exec"
      - "#warp-state"
      - "#lift-code-units"

  sem-delta:
    description: >-
      The SemDelta and diff(base, branch), keyed by stableKey (SymbolEntry.id, so
      rename survives). contentId-equal short-circuits to no-delta; a pure
      rename/move is the EMPTY delta. Classifies changed contract slots and edge
      add/remove. A symbol-born delta carries an edgesAdded-only changeset (ALL of
      the born object's outgoing edges) so a new symbol's outgoing edges feed the
      dangle pass the same way a gained edge does (born-consumer → retired-target).
      STAGE 3b: ContractChangeset gains a bodyChanged flag → a 'body' slot in
      changedSlotsOf. classify sets bodyChanged when either side is a code-unit:
      classify runs ONLY when contentIds already differ, and a code-unit's identity
      IS its body (incl. inline-substituted local refs), so a differing code-unit
      contentId means the body moved. 'body' is the body-INTERNAL analog of 'steps'
      (a literal/operator change with no call change fires 'body' but not 'edges'; a
      call add/remove still fires 'edges' via the existing path — both correct). This
      closes the TD-806 "0 deltas on a .ts-only change" gap. RANKING SIGNAL
      (T-2026-07-03-002, additive): each delta also carries localChanged — TRUE iff
      THIS side changed the symbol's OWN content (born/retired existence, a
      name-keyed slot/edge, a scalar contract field, or a code-unit's raw
      codeEssence/codeLocalTargets), FALSE = a pure RIPPLE re-address (contentId
      moved only via edge-target essence transitivity, Merkle-by-target). Never
      consulted by knot/dangle/autoClean semantics — presentation+ranking data only.
    type: module

  predict:
    description: >-
      predict(ΔA, ΔB) -> {autoClean, knots[], dangling[]}. The commute / knot /
      dangle decision procedure. Dangle wins precedence so the score is a true
      partition; same-key identical-essence is convergent (autoClean). STAGE 3b:
      'body' joins the scalar-conflict-slot list (componentType|kind|steps|body) so
      two branches that edit the SAME code-unit's body to DIFFERENT essences are a
      KNOT with conflictingSlots ['body'] (git may auto-merge textually-distant
      edits Warpline flags at function granularity); a call to a code-unit the other
      branch retired surfaces as a DANGLE via the unchanged edge pass. RANKING
      (T-2026-07-03-002, additive): Knot.direct / Dangle.direct = TRUE iff ≥1 side's
      delta has localChanged (own-content edit); FALSE = a ripple-only knot (both
      essences shifted transitively — the 48-176-symbol avalanche class ground truth
      proved 0% churn-validated). Absent flags default direct (surfaced, never
      silently collapsed). The knot/dangle DECISIONS themselves are unchanged.
      RULE LABELS (refusal:v1 SP1a, TD-2026-07-21-766): every emitted Knot and
      Dangle carries KnotRule — which of the five branches fired (retire-vs-edit
      | born-divergent | both-retype | conflicting-slot | dangle-retire). The
      former boolean isKnot predicate became knotRuleOf, returning WHICH branch
      fired instead of merely THAT one did (non-null is exactly the old true), so
      the partition is byte-identical: this labels control flow, it does not
      steer it. The label is what lets a cold agent (falsifier F4) branch on WHY
      without parsing prose — retire-vs-edit is a keep-or-drop decision,
      conflicting-slot is a merge-the-slot decision, born-divergent is a pick-one
      decision: same verdict class, different work. OPTIONAL on the interface so
      hand-built Prediction fixtures and pre-rule persisted shapes stay valid;
      absent means UNLABELLED, never a guessed label (#refusal reports it as
      null). Consumed by #refusal's contested index.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#sem-delta"

  justification:
    description: >-
      The v0 Justification — actor/intent from the branch tip's git log,
      computedRipple via getReferencesTo over changed symbols, danglingRefs, and a
      reserved signature field (unsigned:sha256 stub). P2.3 (forge-spec §3b,
      ADDITIVE): an optional claimId POINTER to the author's pre-declared #claim
      sidecar — present only when the change was proposed under a claim (absent ⇒
      byte-identical to the pre-claim schema); the claim body and its grading
      never ride inline (G5).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#git-exec"

  strand:
    description: >-
      A Strand — one PICK sealed into the fabric. Two deliberately-separated
      identities: stateId (the REPRODUCIBLE content-address of the meaning it lands
      on, from #warp-state) and pickId (the EVENT identity, including actor + time +
      intent, so attribution/provenance lives here). Carries a compact StrandDelta
      (born/retired/contractChanged names + renamedNoop count), an objectCount, git
      coexistence provenance (ref/treeSha/gitCommit), and calibratedConfidence —
      RESERVED (null until graded), the one field a git-backed history can't carry
      and the seed of the non-portable moat. computePickId null-normalizes the body
      (canonical rejects null) for the ADDRESS only; stored JSON keeps real nulls.
      SCHEMA v2 (TD-2026-07-01-721, the pickId identity contract): schemaVersion
      widens 1→(1|2). A v2 pickId is pick:v2:sha256 and folds INTO the address the
      chain link (parentPickId = the ledger tip's pickId, so reorder/forge breaks the
      chain, A1), the byte binding (binding.treeId, so a bait-and-switch binding is
      caught without a signature — Aegis C2), agent attribution (authoredBy.agentId;
      sessionKey EXCLUDED), and on a merge the second DAG parent (mergeParentPickId =
      the base strand) + the pinned merge algo (MergeRecipe.algo 'warpline-merge3-v1',
      restored per Judge). Still EXCLUDED from both rules: calibratedConfidence,
      binding.gitOid, merge.{base,ours,theirs,result} — and binding.treeSemantics
      (T-2026-07-18-005: the additive tree-semantics tag, 'worktree:v1' on new
      bindings, absent = legacy-git; rides OUTSIDE the preimage in BOTH epochs, so
      no signed identity moved). strand.byteOnly (T-2026-07-18-002, the
      byte-custody flag on a meaning-NOOP + tree-advanced seal) is additive and
      rides INTO the v2 preimage via the rest spread (new strands only;
      derivable: empty delta + stateId === parent's + binding advanced). The v1
      rule (pick:v0) is byte-for-byte preserved so historical strands re-verify. LEGACY (§7,
      TD-2026-07-01-202): the on-disk fabric was sealed under two retired rules and
      #grade overwrote a hashed field for the oldest strands; computePickIdWholeBody
      + reproducesUnderKnownRule try the finite set of known rules, and the
      graded-over residue is grandfathered by pickId in .warpline/fabric-legacy.json.
      M1b — the byte seam: #pick/#admit/#seal stamp binding on every seal
      (snapshotState = snapshotDir for worktree, snapshotRef for a git ref); a CLEAN
      merge stamps the compositional result (A2 — base tree via cat-file + the merge's
      byte changes, never the git-archive dir).
      SCHEMA v3 (V3.1, docs/specs/warpline-v3-identity.md §1, founder-signed §9):
      schemaVersion widens to (1|2|3). A v3 pickId is pick:v3:H(parents + content) —
      NO ledger position, EVER: seq/parentStateId/merged/parentPickId/
      mergeParentPickId are GONE from the strand (position/merged-ness are DERIVED,
      #fabric-dag); parents: string[] is the ORDERED multi-parent chain link (IN the
      hash — ancestry is identity, so reseal-on-retry mints a NEW id, §1.3); the
      merge recipe folds WHOLE (closes MED-D structurally); binding.treeId folds in
      and is MANDATORY at seal (buildStrandV3 refuses unbound — the HIGH-A class
      dies at the schema level); calibratedConfidence does not exist on a v3 strand
      AT ALL (§7 R4 — grades.jsonl sidecar is authoritative); recordedAt stays IN
      (§9.5); sessionKey stays OUT. The v3 preimage is built EXPLICITLY (no
      rest-spread) so a stray positional field can never move a v3 identity.
      buildStrandV3(input) is the only v3 constructor (pure — the default seal-path
      cutover is V3.3). A sealed v3 strand has ZERO post-seal-mutable fields.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#canonical-json"
      - "#warp-state"
      - "#warp-store"

  seal:
    description: >-
      The single place a pre-absorbed WarpState becomes a fabric #strand and the
      selvage advances. sealState(root, store, state, input) — shared by #pick,
      #admit (merge seal), and #resolve (KNOT-council seal) — owns the strand
      schema, the seq, the delta summary (summarizeDelta computed BEFORE putState
      so the dedup case where current.stateId === parent.stateId doesn't diff
      against itself), and the optional `resolves` envelope, so there is ONE writer
      of fabric history. Publishes ledger-first then a CAS'd selvage advance (only
      if the tip is still the parent the decision was based on) — defense-in-depth
      under #fabric-lock so a lost-update can never silently write a wrong tip.
      REFS MODE (V3.2): once a repo has run the one-time `warpline refs migrate`,
      seal DUAL-WRITES — the legacy stateId selvage AND refs/heads/selvage (the tip
      pickId) advance together via #fabric-refs writeRef per-ref CAS; drifted tip
      pointers refuse the seal fail-closed. Unmigrated repos are byte-for-byte
      untouched (seal never creates refs/heads as a side effect).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#fabric"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#fabric-refs"

  grade:
    description: >-
      The calibration grader (Loid's MOAT — the one thing a git-backed history can't
      carry). admit SEEDS strand.calibratedConfidence from the gate rule; gradeFabric
      grades it against real OUTCOME, mirroring the classroom certify→outcome loop. A
      strand's AUTHORED symbols (delta.born ∪ contractChanged) are OVERTURNED when a
      later strand RETIRES one (or a #resolve names it in resolves.contended) →
      confidence ↓; SURVIVED when they hold across a window of later strands → ↑;
      PENDING when too recent. applyGrades updates calibratedConfidence in the ledger
      (pickId EXCLUDES the field, so the content-address is unchanged) and appends the
      trajectory to .warpline/grades.jsonl, under #fabric-lock. The report buckets
      survival by gate-rule prior class — the falsifiable 'does linked beat
      independent?' moat experiment. V3 (v3-identity §7, R4): a v3 strand carries NO
      calibratedConfidence at all — applyGrades never rewrites a v3 strand; its
      grade trajectory lives ONLY in grades.jsonl (the sidecar becomes fully
      authoritative at V3.4, when rewriteFabric is deleted). P3 LANE A2 (forge-spec
      §1d): grades are additionally KEYED by WHO and WHAT — every StrandGrade (and
      thus every grades.jsonl row) carries authoredBy.agentId + the authoredSymbols
      set, and GradeReport gains byAgent/bySymbol buckets (per-symbol outcome is
      conservative: a non-overturned symbol on an OVERTURNED strand is not graded,
      never silently 'survived'). The module also hosts the sidecar's FIRST
      CONSUMER: readGradeSidecar → symbolSurvivalIndex (latest row per pickId wins
      — the sidecar is a trajectory) → evaluateEscalation, a PURE function of
      (decision, sidecar snapshot) implementing the one trust-floor rule for
      #admit's HELD verdict; constants K_MIN_GRADED=3 / SURVIVAL_FLOOR=0.5 are
      exported STARTING GUESSES, calibration-pending (TD-2026-07-16-426 organic
      arm — moved only by graded outcome data, never hand-tuned). Accepted-risk
      overrides append to .warpline/grades-escalations.jsonl (G5 sidecar;
      recordGradeEscalation/listGradeEscalations).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#fabric"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#strand"

  fabric-lock:
    description: >-
      A cross-process advisory lock around the fabric's read-decide-write critical
      section (Reviewer C1). admit/pick/resolve — and the post-commit auto-seal hook
      which BACKGROUNDS its seal — can otherwise interleave readSelvage→…→writeSelvage
      and lose a write (a strand appended that isn't the tip, two strands sharing a
      seq). withFabricLock (acquireFabricLock/releaseFabricLock) acquires an O_EXCL
      lockfile under .warpline/refs/.lock (atomic create = acquire), bounded retry
      with backoff, and steals a stale lock (crashed holder) after a TTL. OWNERSHIP
      (M2 item 4, Judge): the lockfile carries an OWNER TOKEN (pid+random) written
      at acquire; release unlinks ONLY if the on-disk token is still ours — after a
      steal the old holder's release leaves the new holder's lock alone (no
      cascading multi-holder); the steal CLAIMS the stale file by atomic
      rename-aside (exactly one stealer wins; never a blind unlink). The in-process
      #repo-lock only guards the git merge-tree fallback; this is the on-disk lock
      the hook's cross-process seals need. Paired with #fabric's rewriteFabric
      LOST-UPDATE CAS: the on-disk ledger must still be pick-for-pick the one the
      rewrite was derived from, else throw — racing an append never drops a strand.
    type: module

  resolve:
    description: >-
      The KNOT council. resolveKnot(root, {agentId, resolvedRef, reason, decidedBy,
      oursRef?}) seals a HUMAN resolution of a genuine conflict #admit refused to
      auto-merge: it absorbs the resolved state, records a #strand `resolves`
      envelope (decidedBy, reason, the contended symbols — precise when --ours is
      supplied so the original knot is recomputed — and the symbols changed vs the
      tip), seals via #seal, advances the selvage, and clears the scratch. The
      reasoning git's merge commit can't keep lives natively in the fabric.
      P2.2: also accepts a #knot-payload resolution proposal — proposalToResolveOptions
      maps a verified knotResolutionProposal:v1 (enveloped reason, resolvedRef,
      decidedBy) 1:1 onto ResolveOptions; sealing stays behind this verb (G4).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#admit"
      - "#seal"
      - "#fabric-lock"

  fabric:
    description: >-
      The durable Warpline history of this project — the append-only PICK-DAG
      ledger (.warpline/fabric.jsonl) + the live tip pointer (.warpline/refs/selvage,
      published atomically via write-tmp+rename so a crash never leaves a
      half-written tip). readSelvage/writeSelvage/appendStrand/readFabric.
      COEXISTENCE INVARIANT: writes ONLY under .warpline/ — never the user's git
      HEAD/index/worktree — extending the Phase-1 ~read-only disk boundary from a
      debug cache into the authoritative store. Git keeps running normally;
      Warpline accumulates its OWN meaning-history alongside it.
      FAIL-CLOSED READS: readSelvage/readFabric swallow ONLY ENOENT (genuinely
      never-sealed ⇒ null/[]). A present-but-unreadable tip, or a malformed ledger
      line, THROWS (with the corrupt line's position) — an authoritative store must
      never read corruption as "empty history", which would let #admit fast-admit a
      fresh genesis over a real tip (silent data loss). Correspondingly #admit fails
      closed when the selvage/base points at a stateId whose state cannot be loaded.
      rewriteFabric carries the §4.4/§7.4 IDENTITY GUARD: it recomputes every strand's
      pickId (trying the known rules via reproducesUnderKnownRule) and refuses to write
      unless each reproduces OR it is a GRANDFATHERED v1 strand whose PINNED bodyHash
      still matches (HIGH-2 containment — the grandfathered path no longer skips the
      guard wholesale; only a calibratedConfidence change is legal). The legacy
      manifest (.warpline/fabric-legacy.json, readLegacyGrandfathered) is BODY-PINNED
      {pickId, bodyHash} where bodyHash = computeLegacyBodyHash (canonical body
      excluding calibratedConfidence/binding/merge — the v1 exclusion set, so #grade
      and `objects backfill` stay legal); the retired bare-pickId format FAILS CLOSED
      with a regenerate instruction (never auto-upgraded — an unpinned grandfather
      clause is the hole this closed). So #grade may move calibratedConfidence
      (excluded from the hash) but can never silently drift an identity field.
      V1 FREEZE (#anchor, docs/specs/warpline-v1-anchor.md §7): once the ON-DISK
      fabric carries an epoch anchor, rewriteFabric refuses ALL v1 mutation — grading,
      binding stamps, repair — via a byte-level full-strand digest compare against the
      on-disk prefix (closes the HIGH-A binding-injection WRITE path at the writer;
      #grade skips frozen v1 strands, their trajectory living in grades.jsonl only).
      V3 IMMUTABILITY (v3-identity §1.1): rewriteFabric refuses ANY byte difference
      on a v3 strand (zero post-seal-mutable fields — a writer can't even GRAFT a
      calibratedConfidence on); rewriteFabric itself is scheduled for deletion at
      V3.4. The tip pointer grows a refs-mode sibling: refs/heads/selvage (a pickId,
      #fabric-refs) coexists with the legacy stateId selvage during the cutover.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#strand"

  fabric-verify:
    description: >-
      warpline fabric verify — authenticate the whole PICK-DAG (verifyFabric(root) ->
      FabricVerifyReport), read-only. Per strand in seq order: (1) INTEGRITY —
      recompute pickId trying the finite set of known hashing rules (§7.1: the v2
      rule, or for v1 the current-exclusion rule OR the retired whole-body rule) and
      accept on any match; (2) CHAIN (v2) — parentPickId == the immediately-preceding
      strand's pickId (null at a v2 genesis, the v1 TIP at the boundary), else
      chain-break; (3) MERGE (v2) — mergeParentPickId resolves to an earlier strand,
      else merge-parent-unresolved; (4) BINDING — every tree/blob reachable from
      binding.treeId RE-HASHES to its content-address (recompute via the store's
      verified reads, NOT mere presence — MEDIUM-1; memoized so shared subtrees hash
      once; a self-consistent DANGLING binding is the backstop for a re-forged
      pickId), else missing-binding / corrupt-object / binding-mismatch; (5) MERGE
      RECIPE (the OQ-D compensating control — recipe fields are EXCLUDED from the
      pickId by design, so verify is the authority): all four recipe trees
      ({base,ours,theirs,result}) exist AND recompute, and merge.result ===
      binding.treeId, else merge-recipe-invalid. Classification (§7.3 + HIGH-2
      containment): known-rule match -> OK; no match + a V1 strand grandfathered
      AND matching its PINNED bodyHash -> legacy-unverifiable (SOFT, exit 0); a
      grandfathered strand whose BODY moved -> legacy-body-mismatch (HARD — the
      clause exempts the retired pickId rule, never the body); no match + not
      grandfathered -> pickId-mismatch (HARD) — how a real forgery still surfaces.
      MANIFEST SANITY: every fabric-legacy.json entry must correspond to an existing
      v1 strand; an unknown entry or a v2 pickId in the list -> legacy-manifest-
      invalid (HARD, seq -1). Report carries v1Prefix{count,selfHashOk},
      v2Chain{count,ok}, boundaryAnchored, legacyUnverifiable{count,pickIds},
      failures[]. Exit 0 intact / 1 tamper / 2 I/O error. Distinct from `objects
      verify` (loose-object self-consistency). Spec
      docs/specs/warpline-fabric-schema-v2.md (+ the 2026-07-01 amendment).
      V3 DAG WALK (V3.1, v3-identity §3.1–4): v3 strands skip the linear chain check
      and get (3.2) CLOSURE — every parents[] entry resolves in-fabric, else
      parent-unresolved (HARD, truncation/forgery evidence); (3.3) CAUSALITY —
      parent line-index < child line-index (the causal-append invariant), else
      causality-violation; (3.4) acyclicity (dag-cycle — impossible under honest
      hashing, so forgery evidence) + at most one parentless genesis
      (multiple-genesis) + MANDATORY binding (missing-binding — no unbound v3
      strand is legal). Report adds v3Dag{count,ok}. REFS (V3.2, §3.5): every
      refs/heads/* entry resolves to a present strand else ref-unresolved (HARD);
      headless tips no ref names land in report.abandonedHeads (REPORTED, never a
      failure); in refs mode the legacy selvage is cross-checked against the
      selvage REF's strand (not the physical tip). A v3 failure's `seq` is the
      LINE INDEX (v3 stores no seq). The §3.7 nested-epoch anchor walk is V3.3.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#strand"
      - "#fabric"
      - "#fabric-dag"
      - "#fabric-refs"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#anchor"

  fabric-dag:
    description: >-
      The DERIVED DAG index over the fabric ledger (V3.1, v3-identity §1.2 + §4).
      fabric.jsonl is an ARRIVAL LOG (a local receipt), not the authority on order —
      the DAG in the strands is. parentsOf() unifies the epochs (v3 parents[]; v2
      parentPickId+mergeParentPickId; v1 none). buildDag(strands) → byPickId,
      children, the deterministic topological ORDER (ties between concurrent strands
      broken by (recordedAt, pickId) ascending — deterministic across machines GIVEN
      THE SAME STRAND SET, the correct promise), positionOf, heads (tips with no
      children), roots, missingParents (closure holes — verify makes them HARD; the
      index still orders the remainder for display), and cycle (unorderable strands —
      forgery evidence under honest hashing). Duplicate appends dedup (content-
      addressed, §1.3 idempotence). A derived position may be SHOWN (the @N selector
      sugar) but is never persisted and never a durable selector across exchange.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#strand"

  fabric-refs:
    description: >-
      pickId refs + per-ref CAS (V3.2, v3-identity §2, founder-signed §9.1: refs
      hold PICKIDS, not stateIds — a stateId is many-to-one and cannot name a
      history position). .warpline/refs/heads/<name> each hold an event identity;
      readRef/writeRef (atomic tmp+rename; CAS refuses when the on-disk ref moved —
      verbatim the writeSelvage mechanics re-scoped per ref; a losing writer reseal-
      retries or publishes under its own ref and weaves later, jj/darcs posture),
      listRefs, heads() — the API new code writes against instead of assuming a
      unique tip (spec §6.3; legacy fallback = the single ledger tip). Ref names are
      single-segment validated fail-closed (spliced into a filesystem path — no
      traversal). migrateSelvageToRefs is the ONE-TIME founder-visible migration:
      resolves the legacy stateId selvage via the highest-seq hack for the LAST
      time, writes refs/heads/selvage, idempotent, fails closed on a corrupt tip —
      NEVER automatic (unmigrated repos, including the live dogfood fabric until
      its own step, stay untouched). The O_EXCL #fabric-lock survives as the short
      critical-section guard for append+ref-write.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#fabric"
      - "#strand"

  anchor:
    description: >-
      The v1-prefix EPOCH ANCHOR (docs/specs/warpline-v1-anchor.md) — closes the
      HIGH-A / HIGH-B / MED-C live exploits (the v2 chain was authenticated, the v1
      prefix was not). computePrefixDigest folds the FULL v1 strands (bodies,
      bindings, pickIds all included) domain-separated + length-prefixed; the anchor
      is a NORMAL v2 strand carrying an `attests` block (EpochAnchor) that rides into
      its pickId via computePickId's ...rest spread — so the attestation is itself
      chain-protected. `warpline fabric attest` (attestFabric) is the ONE-TIME verb:
      refuse-if-attested → precondition verify (coverage suppressed) → corroborate the
      working prefix+manifest against git history of .warpline/fabric.jsonl (record the
      matching commit) → seal a meaning-no-op anchor at the tip. No re-attest/force/
      repair verb exists (a re-attest path = tamper-then-re-attest). fabric-verify then
      authenticates the prefix against the anchor (anchor-missing/mismatch/manifest-
      mismatch/duplicate/malformed, v1-out-of-prefix; + the §6.7 selvage/tip cross-check)
      and REQUIRES coverage as a code constant. assertV1Covered is the cheap restore
      gate. Residual (stated honestly): a full re-chain by an in-loop writer stays
      possible — tamper-EVIDENT, not trustworthy; signatures are M3.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#strand"
      - "#fabric"
      - "#seal"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#git-exec"

  backfill:
    description: >-
      `warpline objects backfill` (backfillV1Bindings) — stamp a native byte binding
      onto every v1 strand lacking one, from its provenance.gitCommit tree
      (snapshotRef), so the v1 prefix has all the bindings it will EVER have BEFORE the
      epoch anchor freezes it. gitOid:null deliberately (MED-E — a backfilled native
      tree is ignore-filtered, so the git sha would be a lie; the check is vacuous for
      self-produced values). Binding is EXCLUDED from the v1 pickId, so a stamp is
      legal pre-anchor. A strand whose gitCommit is null/unreachable stays UNBOUND
      forever (restore refuses permanently, A4). PRE-ATTESTATION ONLY: refuses once an
      anchor exists (findAnchor) — the same one-way freeze gate #fabric's rewriteFabric
      keys off. Ordering trap: backfill → git commit → attest must not be reordered.
      DELTA-NATIVE (T-2026-07-04-003): snapshots CHAIN — each strand anchors on the
      previous one just computed, so a long v1 prefix costs N diffs, not N universes.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#fabric"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#anchor"

  restore:
    description: >-
      The layer→VCS THRESHOLD (M1c, T-2026-07-01-012) — reconstruct a working tree
      from the NATIVE object store with git ABSENT. Two modules + a hardened writer:
      select.ts (resolveSelector(wdir, selector) → treeId): omitted|HEAD|selvage →
      the tip strand — refs mode (V3.2) resolves the EXACT pickId at
      refs/heads/selvage (no stateId ambiguity); legacy mode keeps the
      latest-arrival-on-stateId disambiguation (grandfathered until migration);
      N|@N → stored seq === N, falling back to the DERIVED topo position
      (#fabric-dag) when the fabric carries position-free v3 strands (local sugar,
      never durable); pick:<id> → that pickId; state:<id> → the most-recent-arrival
      strand on that stateId (§3 many-to-one); tree:<id> → that treeId DIRECTLY
      (no strand). A4
      (cutover-refuse-on-missing-binding): a resolved strand with no binding.treeId
      (sealed pre-M1b) is REFUSED loudly — never silently restored empty. restore.ts
      (restore(root, {selector, to, force})): the DIRTY-DEST GUARD refuses a non-empty
      dest without --force; --force OVERLAYS (overwrites colliding regular files,
      leaves unrelated files, never wipes). The byte writer restoreTree (#warp-store,
      snapshot.ts) is PATH-HARDENED (Aegis C3, CVE-2021-21300 class): it FAILS CLOSED
      (throws, aborting the whole restore) on a forged/corrupt entry name —
      assertSafeEntryName rejects empty/./../slash/backslash/NUL/absolute/drive-letter
      and the reserved .git/.warpline; the parent dir must be a real (non-symlink)
      directory; and it never writes THROUGH a pre-existing symlink (traversal escape).
      Byte-faithful modes preserved (100644/100755/120000/40000/160000). Proof:
      rm -rf .git && warpline restore HEAD reproduces the exact tree (external), and
      snapshot(restore(x)) == x.binding.treeId (internal round-trip).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#fabric"
      - "#strand"

  pick:
    description: >-
      The single-writer WRITE PATH (Phase 2). recordPick(root, opts) absorbs the
      working tree (or a ref) to a WarpState, and — using the DIFF vs the parent
      selvage as the source of truth for "did meaning change?" (NOT stateId
      equality, which hashes the DEDUPED essence set and so misses a born symbol
      whose essence equals an existing one) — decides NOOP vs seal, then seals a
      #strand via #seal (the single fabric writer) under #fabric-lock (so a
      backgrounded post-commit auto-seal can't race it). BYTE CUSTODY
      (T-2026-07-18-002): NOOP ⟺ empty deltas AND empty renames AND tree
      unchanged (vs the tip strand's binding, under the canonical worktree
      semantics — T-2026-07-18-005); a meaning-NOOP whose TREE advanced seals a
      BYTE-CUSTODY strand instead (strand.byteOnly:true additive, IN the v2
      pickId via the rest spread; stateId naturally equals the parent's; the
      binding advances) so doc/lore/config-only commits keep durable native
      custody (git-absent restorable; auto-stake counts them). Byte-only is
      always FAST/no-gate: a NOOP verdict never refuses, even agent-attributed
      under gate:'real'. New bindings are stamped
      binding.treeSemantics:'worktree:v1'. Genesis (no selvage)
      records seq 0 with an empty delta; objectCount is the headline. FAIL-CLOSED
      (T-029): a SET selvage whose parent state cannot be loaded now THROWS rather
      than falling through to seal (which would orphan history) — symmetric with the
      #admit guard. SCHEMA v2: RecordPickOptions gains agentId/sessionKey; the seal
      stamps authoredBy (agentId IN the pickId, sessionKey excluded). No agentId (the
      hook/human path) → authoredBy.agentId null. The single-writer daily-driver path
      — the multi-writer protocol is #admit. DELTA-NATIVE (T-2026-07-04-003): a REF
      pick's seal snapshot anchors on the tip strand's verified binding
      (strandSnapshotAnchor) — the post-commit hook seal costs one commit's diff, not
      the whole tree; worktree picks keep the full snapshotDir walk, unchanged.
      NATIVE-FIRST R1: when #warpline-config sets shadowGate:true, every pick ALSO
      records the #shadow-gate observe-only verdict of the just-lifted state vs the
      PRE-seal selvage (reusing the absorb — pure compute). FAIL-SAFE: shadow is
      telemetry; a shadow failure never blocks or fails the seal. NATIVE-FIRST R2
      (T-2026-07-18-001, loid-loops.md R2 "mixed mode"): gate.agentWrites:'real'
      makes an AGENT-ATTRIBUTED pick (agentId present — --agent /
      $WARPLINE_AGENT_ID, incl. the auto-seal hook forwarding it) ENFORCE that
      same verdict: would-not-seal (KNOT/DANGLE/HELD/non-materializable CLEAN)
      REFUSES the seal (PickGateRefusal; row records gate:'real'), override =
      `pick --accept-risk` (row records overridden:true — never silent).
      FAIL-CLOSED for agents (an enforced gate that crashes, or a config too
      corrupt to read, refuses); humans/unattributed picks stay byte-identical
      (two doors, one fabric). After a successful non-noop seal the R2
      auto-stake cadence (#stake maybeAutoStakeOnSeal) runs best-effort,
      outside the fabric lock.
      REFUSAL (refusal:v1 SP1c, ADDITIVE): PickGateRefusal now carries a
      `.refusal` — the machine-readable #refusal beside the human `message`.
      The would-not-seal refusal re-homes the UNDERLYING #admit refusal (from the
      shadow pipeline) to gate:'pick', keeping its code, ranked contested index,
      pointers and override door, and rewrites next[] for the pick surface —
      because the verb that retries HERE is `pick`, not `admit`. Its ladder omits
      knot.show by design: the gate's verdict comes from #shadow-gate, which
      builds a #knot-payload as pipeline proof and never persists it, so there is
      no selector to hand out; the durable artifact is the VERDICT ROW, read back
      via shadow.tail. The two fail-CLOSED throws are labelled too — a corrupt
      config yields code ENGINE with retriable 'never' and an EMPTY next[] (the
      machine-readable way to say "no call recovers this; escalate"), while a
      failed verdict pipeline yields ENGINE with 'retry-identical' (the request
      was well-formed; lock contention is the common cause). Constructor arg is
      optional so external callers still compile; every throw site here populates
      it.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#refusal"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#seal"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#git-exec"
      - "#warpline-config"
      - "#shadow-gate"
      - "#stake"

  scratch:
    description: >-
      A per-agent ephemeral view (the read side of multi-writer). forkScratch(agentId)
      pins the stateId the agent works against (its optimistic base) under
      .warpline/refs/scratch/<agentId>; N agents fork the SAME selvage concurrently
      with zero contention — what git's single shared working-tree/index/HEAD cannot
      do. Forking is O(1), no working-tree lock. readScratch/clearScratch round it out.
      NATIVE-FIRST (phase 0, I9): the same file doubles as the native SCRATCH REF —
      writeScratchRef stores a PICKID (`warpline fork` mints it at the selvage tip;
      #native-write-path propose advances it per sealed scratch strand). Consumers
      dispatch on the pick:/state: prefix; a mismatch fails closed, never coerces.
      CAS (PW-10, optional `expect` param): a conditional write throws on
      current≠expected instead of silently clobbering — the loud-failure half
      of the fork clobber guard (two same-principal sessions could orphan each
      other's sealed proposal with no error anywhere).
    type: module

  admit:
    description: >-
      The multi-writer WRITE side (Phase C v1, DECISION-level). admitDecision(base,
      proposed, selvage) is the pure verdict: NOOP (no meaning change — decided by
      diff(base, proposed) being empty, NOT stateId equality, same rule as #pick:
      stateId hashes the DEDUPED essence set and misses a change whose before/after
      essences both pre-exist elsewhere); FAST_ADMIT
      (selvage unadvanced since base → admit directly); CLEAN (selvage advanced but
      predict(agentΔ, otherΔ) is autoClean — the concurrent edits commute in MEANING,
      the case git CONFLICTS on for textually-adjacent edits — carrying a GATE-RULE
      confidence: 'linked' when the two changed-sets are dependency-adjacent in-graph
      (Merkle would surface a real conflict, so autoClean is trustworthy) vs
      'independent' when disjoint (autoClean may HIDE a cross-symbol semantic conflict
      per the false-AUTOFOLD gate)); KNOT (same symbol, contradictory meaning → human
      DECIDE, never silently merged); DANGLE (edge into a retired symbol). v2: admit()
      now PERFORMS the merge — on FAST_ADMIT it seals the proposed state; on CLEAN it
      calls #materialize to produce the merged tree, seals it, advances selvage, and
      clears the scratch (if materialization finds a byte conflict the meaning layer
      missed, it downgrades to KNOT — never a silent wrong-merge). Materialization
      needs git refs for ours/base/theirs (the strands' provenance.gitCommit anchors);
      a WORKTREE proposal returns CLEAN unsealed. A CLEAN re-base onto a MERGE strand
      also fails CLOSED (unsealed): a merge strand's gitCommit is ONE parent and lacks
      the merged bytes, so re-basing off it would mis-materialize a 3rd-gen merge (H1).
      SCHEMA v2: all three seal calls pass authoredBy {agentId: opts.agentId}; the
      CLEAN merge seal also passes mergeParentPickId = the base strand's pickId (the
      SECOND DAG parent), folded with merge.algo into the authenticated v2 pickId.
      DELTA-NATIVE (T-2026-07-04-003): every seal-time snapshot is ANCHORED where
      verifiable — FAST_ADMIT anchors on the tip strand (selvage === base there),
      the CLEAN capture anchors base on the base strand's verified binding (ours/
      theirs then diff off the base tree), and the H1-relaxed native path anchors
      each git-ref side on its strand binding — so a warm admit on the full
      monorepo runs in single-digit SECONDS (was >2min; bench/bench-admit.mts).
      Anchors are verified by #warp-store's strandSnapshotAnchor and fail OPEN to
      the full snapshot; verdict semantics are untouched (decision before bytes).
      P2.2 (forge-spec §3a): a meaning-level KNOT/DANGLE verdict additionally
      durably snapshots the unsealed proposal's bytes, builds the machine-readable
      #knot-payload, persists it to .warpline/knots/, and returns knotPayloadId —
      a POINTER on AdmitResult (G1-additive; never the payload inline). Payload
      construction is derived evidence: a build failure degrades to a verdict
      without a pointer, never a crash. The verdict itself remains a pure function
      of STRUCTURAL inputs — prose never reaches admitDecision (the poisoned-prose
      invariant, test/injection-envelope.test.ts). P2.3 (forge-spec §3b): admit
      optionally judges the admission against a pre-declared #claim
      (AdmitOptions.claim = a claimId from the propose API) — the CLAIM GATE runs
      strictly BEFORE any seal: a breach (computed touched ⊄ claimed, per #claim's
      documented excess rule) returns the CLAIM-BREACH verdict class (G1-additive
      to AdmitStatus; never returned by admitDecision itself) — HELD, unsealed,
      with the exact excess/missing sets and the underlying verdict on
      AdmitResult.claim. FAIL-SAFE not fail-hard: acceptBreach is the explicit
      override that seals the underlying verdict while recording the breach fact.
      Every judgment (breach or not, sealed or not) appends a row to
      .warpline/claims/evaluations.jsonl (the calibration-probe stream, G5).
      Claims are OPT-IN: an admit without a claim is byte-identical to pre-claim
      behavior (tested), and no claim ever narrows what admit computes (the perf
      hint is recorded-only). Strand/pickId identity is UNTOUCHED — the claim
      pointer lives in sidecars and the Justification, never the signed strand.
      P3 GAP-1 (G1-additive): a materialized CLEAN admit also attaches
      AdmitResult.coverage — the #honesty per-path labels (meaning-decided /
      byte-decided / derived) + aggregate counts over the merge plan's changed
      paths. Presentation data, never a verdict input. P3 LANE A2 (forge-spec §1d
      — the permission model IS the scrutiny policy): the TRUST-FLOOR gate, the
      grades sidecar's first consumer. After the claim gate (claims judge honesty
      before trust is), an independent-confidence CLEAN — the autoClean class the
      false-AUTOFOLD gate proved blind — touching a symbol whose GRADED survival
      (#grade's symbolSurvivalIndex over grades.jsonl; ≥ K_MIN_GRADED graded
      outcomes, MIN across touched symbols) is below SURVIVAL_FLOOR returns the
      HELD verdict class (G1-additive to AdmitStatus; never returned by
      admitDecision): refused, unsealed, selvage unmoved, with
      AdmitResult.escalation naming the symbol + survival + n. Same fail-safe
      mechanics as CLAIM-BREACH: acceptRisk is the explicit override — it seals
      the underlying CLEAN and records the override row in
      .warpline/grades-escalations.jsonl (G5; never silent). The escalation
      decision is PURE over (decision, sidecar snapshot); the sidecar is read
      ONLY on the independent-CLEAN path, so a repo with no grades data — and
      every other verdict path — behaves byte-identically to before the rule
      (regression-guarded in test/trust-escalation.test.ts).
      NATIVE-FIRST R1 (AdmitOptions.shadow, #shadow-gate): shadow mode runs this
      whole pipeline — claim gate, verdict, escalation, coverage (read-only
      plan), knot-payload BUILD — and swaps every write (seal, snapshot, sidecar
      rows, payload persist) for a plain return; AdmitOptions.proposedState lets
      #pick reuse its own absorb. The decision function is untouched.
      NATIVE-FIRST R2 (T-2026-07-18-001): a SEALED, NON-SHADOW admission
      triggers the config-gated auto-stake cadence (#stake maybeAutoStakeOnSeal)
      best-effort at the single wrap point — never blocking the admission.
      SCHEMA STAMP (refusal:v1 SP0): AdmitResult carries schemaVersion
      'admitResult:v1' (G1) — the result crosses the daemon wire and lands in
      forge/GUI consumers verbatim (G3), so it names its own shape instead of
      being duck-typed. The stamp is applied EXACTLY ONCE at the public boundary
      (admit(); admitNative() mirrors it) over an internal AdmitResultBody, so no
      builder can forget or forge it and every internal return site is unchanged.
      REFUSAL (refusal:v1 SP1c, ADDITIVE): every REFUSING return additionally
      carries AdmitResult.refusal — the machine-readable #refusal. CLAIM-BREACH
      raises code CLAIM_BREACH at gate 'claim' (pointers.symbols = the excess
      set, override acceptBreach); the trust floor raises TRUST_HELD at gate
      'trust' with an honestly EMPTY contested set (the verdict was CLEAN — what
      refused is the floor, so pointers.symbols names the below-floor symbol);
      KNOT/DANGLE raise GATE_REFUSED at gate 'meaning' pointing at the persisted
      #knot-payload, with next[] = knot.show then resolve (human-class per Aegis
      §2.2). The byte-conflict DOWNGRADES (meaning said CLEAN, bytes overlapped)
      raise the same GATE_REFUSED with contestedTotal 0 — which is the honest
      report that no MEANING unit is contested and the conflict lies below this
      layer — because a refusal must never be ABSENT on a refusing verdict, or a
      cold agent learns it cannot rely on the field (F4). Under SHADOW the
      knot-payload id is deliberately withheld from pointers: the payload is
      built as pipeline proof but never persisted, and a pointer that fails to
      dereference is worse than no pointer. Every pre-existing field (status,
      sealed, claim, escalation, knotPayloadId) is unchanged and remains the
      authority — #refusal restates them in one shape an agent can act on.
      The refusal BUILDERS (meaningRefusal / claimRefusal / trustRefusal) are
      exported and shared with #native-write-path (T-2026-07-21-007): both paths
      raise the SAME objects, differing only in the ladder's admit params
      ({ref} git-era, {native:'true'} native) so next[] is copy-paste runnable
      on the surface that raised it.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#refusal"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#predict"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#fabric"
      - "#scratch"
      - "#materialize"
      - "#seal"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#knot-payload"
      - "#claim"
      - "#honesty"
      - "#grade"
      - "#stake"

  claim:
    description: >-
      The claim-scoped propose API, claim:v1 (P2.3, forge-spec §3b,
      T-2026-06-24-018). A claim is the author's PRE-DECLARATION — agentId,
      claimedSymbols, optional taskRef (a structured id/URL, not prose), optional
      claimedContractDelta (the engine's SemDelta shape VERBATIM, G3 — recorded
      for the future grade stream, not evaluated in v1), optional confidence, and
      an ENVELOPED intent (#prose-envelope, §3d) — content-addressed at creation
      (claimId = claim:v1:sha256(canonical body); createClaim is the only
      constructor; verifyClaim recomputes the address + the intent envelope, and
      persist/read fail CLOSED on tamper). The claim schema IS the future OFFER
      metadata (§3b). Triple duty: (1) HONESTY CHECK — evaluateClaim(decision,
      claim) compares claimed vs COMPUTED symbol sets: excess = changed-but-
      unclaimed symbols that are DIRECT (SemDelta.localChanged) or ripple-only-
      but-KNOTTING (a ripple-only non-knotting symbol is exempt — charging authors
      for Merkle avalanches would make honest claims impossible; no agentDelta ⇒
      all treated direct, conservative). The knotting carve-out excludes PROVEN
      body-internal ripples (localChanged false AND rippleFromContract false,
      the #sem-delta stage-1 bit — T-2026-07-21-008): the essence inlines callee
      bodies into callers, so an honest single-symbol edit re-addresses every
      caller and can knot them against a concurrent writer; charging the claim
      for that engine artifact converted accurate self-reporting into a breach
      (the documented trust-erosion path — the CLI's remediation taught agents
      --accept-breach as routine). Exemption exactly as narrow as the proof:
      rippleFromContract absent or true keeps the knot counting (fail closed);
      missing = claimed-but-untouched, recorded
      NEVER a breach; breach = excess nonempty → #admit's CLAIM-BREACH verdict.
      (2) CALIBRATION PROBE — every evaluation appends {claimId, pickId?, agentId,
      breach, excess, missing, acceptedBreach?, ts} to
      .warpline/claims/evaluations.jsonl (G5 sidecar; the per-agent claimed-vs-
      computed honesty signal for the grade layer). (3) PERF HINT — recorded so a
      later layer CAN scope by claim; deliberately not implemented (no decision
      function reads a claim to narrow computation). evaluateClaim reads NO prose
      (the poisoned-prose invariant extends to claim fields). Sidecar persistence
      under .warpline/claims/ keyed by claimId (G2: no ledger positions). Surfaced
      via `warpline propose --agent <id> --claim <file|json>` (returns claimId)
      and `warpline admit … --claim <claimId> [--accept-breach]`.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#prose-envelope"

  prose-envelope:
    description: >-
      The injection-safety envelope (P2.4 minimum slice, forge-spec §3d,
      T-2026-06-24-013). Agent-authored prose (intents, resolution reasons,
      proposal rationales) is UNTRUSTED INPUT to any reading agent/surface: every
      free-text field crossing an agent or rendering boundary is wrapped at
      creation as {kind:'untrusted-prose', contentAddress, body} — born
      content-addressed ('prose:v1:'+sha256), kind tag unforgeable in the shape (a
      body CONTAINING a serialized envelope stays a string). verifyProse recomputes
      the address (tamper ⇒ false); frameProse is the ONLY legal human rendering —
      Warpline authors the frame, control/ANSI bytes are escaped, every body line
      is guttered so no body content can pose as a frame boundary, and a tampered
      envelope refuses to render (fail closed). The third layer — the PURE-FUNCTION
      CONTRACT (gate/verdict decisions read only structural inputs, never prose) —
      is a TESTED invariant: the poisoned-prose suite asserts byte-identical
      verdicts under an adversarial corpus in every prose field.
    type: module

  knot-payload:
    description: >-
      The machine-readable KNOT payload, knotPayload:v1 (P2.2 / R3, forge-spec §3a
      — the forge's issue AND the agent's work order, ONE shape). buildKnotPayload
      turns a KNOT/DANGLE decision + the three states into a SELF-SUFFICIENT
      document: per contested unit both sides' MEANING bodies (the essence-data
      body slot / canonical contract) AND durable file texts read from the
      content-addressed object store, per-side sem-deltas, conflictingSlots,
      direct-vs-ripple, essences; both sides' intents as #prose-envelope
      (untrusted-prose) with agentId/actor attribution; base/ours/theirs stateIds +
      treeIds; the blast-radius as a mode:'ripple' graph slice; and the
      RESOLUTION-PROPOSAL envelope (knotResolutionProposal:v1) that
      proposalToResolveOptions maps 1:1 onto #resolve's ResolveOptions after
      VERIFYING the reason envelope (tamper ⇒ fail closed). Deterministic (no
      clock; payloadId = content address), G1-versioned, G2 (no ledger positions),
      G5 (persisted as derived sidecar data under .warpline/knots/ — never a
      strand). §3a exit gate is a TESTED round-trip: a resolver acting from the
      payload ALONE constructs a resolve call that seals. NO auto-resolution here
      (P5, gated on the blind injection corpus). Surfaced via `warpline knot show
      <payloadId|admitRef>` (--json = the exact engine shape; human view frames
      all prose).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#prose-envelope"
      - "#resolve"

  refusal:
    description: >-
      refusal:v1 — the MACHINE-READABLE REFUSAL every gate hands back (founder
      constraint TD-2026-07-21-766; pre-registered falsifier F4 "cold-agent
      legibility"). THE CONSTRAINT: an agent of ANY provider, with no Warpline in
      its weights and no Warpline docs in context, must complete propose → admit
      → KNOT → resolve using ONLY tool descriptions and the returned refusal
      objects. Git is easy for models because git is MEMORIZED; Warpline never
      will be, so runtime self-description is the only mechanism — and this
      object is its load-bearing carrier. THE BINDING RULE: no human sentences in
      a verdict, ever. Every field is an id, an enum, a pointer or a count;
      human prose stays OUTSIDE the verdict (the thrown Error's message, CLI
      rendering) or ENVELOPED as #prose-envelope untrusted-prose. Fields: code
      (12-value closed enum — GATE_REFUSED / CLAIM_BREACH / TRUST_HELD /
      STALE_BASE / INTEGRITY_BROKEN / AUTH / FORBIDDEN / BAD_REQUEST /
      UNKNOWN_VERB / NOT_FOUND / UNSUPPORTED / ENGINE), verdict (the AdmitStatus
      behind it, null for transport/usage), gate (meaning|claim|trust|pick|
      transport|usage — WHICH layer refused), retriable (retry-identical |
      retry-after-rebase | retry-after-resolve | retry-with-override | never),
      contested[] (the RANKED index — stableKey, symbol, #predict KnotRule,
      conflictingSlots, direct-vs-ripple rank), contestedTotal, pointers
      (knotPayloadId / proposedStateId / rebasedOnto / claimId / symbols — G2:
      never a ledger position), next[] (THE F4 field: verb + already-determined
      params + the arg NAMES still required + principal agent|human, so an agent
      seeing principal:'human' ESCALATES rather than attempts), and an optional
      human-class override door (acceptBreach|acceptRisk). refuse() is the SINGLE
      constructor — no other module builds a Refusal literal, which is what makes
      the cap, the total, the ranking and the code tables INVARIANTS rather than
      per-site conventions. TRUNCATION HONESTY: contested is capped at
      MAX_CONTESTED (32) so an essence-transitivity avalanche cannot blow a
      tool-result budget, contestedTotal always carries the FULL count (a
      truncated list without a total is a lie), and the survivors are the
      direct-contested ones (#rank) so the cap never eats the signal. Every
      pointer must DEREFERENCE: a shadow-built knot payload is never persisted,
      so its id is deliberately withheld rather than advertised as a dead
      knot.show selector. exitCodeFor maps each code to the shell-legible exit
      contract (1 refuse / 2 bad request / 3 claim breach / 4 trust held / 5
      stale base), is TOTAL by construction, and fails CLOSED (1) on an unknown
      code — a refusal must never exit success. exitCodeForResult(result) is the
      whole-OUTCOME form every skin consumes (T-2026-07-21-006: exitCodeFor was
      exported-and-unconsumed, so `admit --native` exited 0 on an unsealed
      CLAIM-BREACH — a silent failure indistinguishable from success): the
      refusal's mapped code when one is present, else 0 — the exit is derived
      from the result's OWN refusal object, never a re-derived vocabulary (G3).
      Deterministic, pure, G1-versioned, G5 (derived data — never a signed
      strand field).
      PW-1 (mcp-skin-spec §4): retriability gains 'retry-corrected' ("a
      corrected or prerequisite call succeeds — follow next[], then retry");
      BAD_REQUEST/UNKNOWN_VERB/NOT_FOUND default to it ('never' on a fixable
      call taught cold agents to abandon instead of correct); AUTH/FORBIDDEN
      stay 'never' (escalation is the door, carried by next[]). RefusedError
      (PW-2 shell) is the THROWN refusal: engine prerequisite boundaries throw
      it carrying a typed refusal:v1, and every skin's catch-all (CLI fail(),
      daemon boundary) detects it and emits the carried refusal verbatim
      instead of collapsing to ENGINE/retry-identical/empty-next — the
      machine hint that instructed an infinite losing retry.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#rank"
      - "#predict"

  rank:
    description: >-
      The DIRECT-CONTESTED vs RIPPLE-ONLY ranking rule, as an engine function
      (T-2026-07-03-002; lifted out of #warpline-cli in the refusal:v1 build).
      rankOf(x) reads #predict's own-content-changed flag — DIRECT iff ≥1 side
      edited the unit's OWN content, RIPPLE iff the essence moved only via
      edge-target transitivity (Merkle-by-target); an ABSENT flag reads as
      DIRECT, so unknown is surfaced and never silently collapsed into the bucket
      consumers are entitled to hide. rankVerdicts(knots) partitions a knot set,
      preserving input order within each bucket. WHY ENGINE, NOT PRESENTATION:
      ground truth over 275 real merges put ≤6-symbol flag sets at 50%
      churn-validated and every ≥10-symbol set — including seven 48-176-symbol
      avalanches rippling out of ~2 genuinely contested units — at 0%, so "which
      of these flags is the actual contest?" is a judgment the engine owes every
      consumer, not a rendering choice one consumer makes. #warpline-cli's
      anti-avalanche display and #refusal's contested cap are now the same
      judgment applied twice (G3). Ranking is DATA ONLY — knot/dangle/autoClean/
      verdict semantics do not move (a policy change would invalidate the
      committed base-rate evidence). Pure and SILENT (cli.ts is the only file
      allowed to write to stdout); parity with the pre-move implementation is a
      TESTED invariant (test/rank.test.ts carries the old code as the oracle).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#predict"

  merge3:
    description: >-
      A token-level 3-way merge (the Phase-C v2 materialization primitive).
      tokenize splits text into word/whitespace/punct runs (join === text);
      merge3(base, ours, theirs) does a hunk-based diff3 over token arrays (LCS
      alignment → change hunks → base-range overlap detection). A range changed by
      only ONE side takes that side; disjoint changes — even two edits on ONE line,
      or a deletion next to an edit — compose cleanly (this is what git's LINE-level
      merge conflicts on). A range BOTH sides changed in OVERLAPPING base coordinates
      is a CONFLICT (counted; merged then unsafe) — NEVER silently resolved (the VCS
      cardinal sin). Pure, no I/O. The O(n·m) LCS is CAPPED — an oversized/minified
      file fails closed (whole-file conflict) instead of OOM-ing (M1).
    type: module

  materialize:
    description: >-
      Phase-C v2 meaning→bytes — PERFORM the merge git could only forecast.
      computeMerge(base, ours, theirs refs) reads each changed file's RAW BYTES via
      git (binary-safe: a binary blob — NUL-bearing — changed on both sides fails
      CLOSED as a conflict, never a UTF-8 text-merge corruption, H3) and merges
      per-file with #merge3; any add/delete-vs-edit
      or overlapping token-region is a surfaced CONFLICT. changedPaths runs with
      `--no-renames` so a rename decomposes to delete-of-old + add-of-new (H2): a
      clean rename applies its delete instead of silently leaving a COPY, and a
      rename racing an edit of the old path fails closed as add/delete-vs-edit. The
      git tree-entry MODE is 3-way-merged alongside the bytes so the executable bit
      survives a content merge; a changed symlink/submodule (non-blob entry) fails
      CLOSED rather than round-trip as a regular blob and corrupt the entry type.
      materializeMergedState applies the conflict-free plan onto a throwaway copy of
      the base tree (restoring each merged file's mode) and absorbs it → the merged
      WarpState (the user's working tree is never touched). gitShow is byte-faithful
      (no trim) so trailing newlines and exact bytes survive — no drift. DERIVED
      ARTIFACTS (P3 GAP-1): a lockfile (#derived-artifacts) changed divergently on
      BOTH sides never conflicts/knots — taken from OURS wholesale (never
      token-merged: a spliced lockfile pretends precision it doesn't have) and
      recorded in MergePlan.derivedStale (additive) for regeneration from the
      merged manifests. Applies identically on the git-ref and native-tree paths.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#merge3"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#git-exec"
      - "#derived-artifacts"

  hook:
    description: >-
      The auto-seal git hook. installHook/uninstallHook/hookStatus manage a
      BEGIN/END-delimited block APPENDED to the repo's post-commit hook (worktree-
      correct path via gitPath) so every git commit also seals a #strand — the
      native history accrues in lockstep with git. Coexists with any existing
      post-commit hook (e.g. Paradigm's history capture), never clobbers it;
      install is idempotent (refreshes the block in place); uninstall strips only
      the warpline block. The block seals `--ref HEAD` (the committed tree, never
      the worktree — so it captures exactly what was committed and never conflates
      another agent's uncommitted work). Runs in the BACKGROUND (a full-repo absorb
      is slow) and is fail-safe (`|| true`) so it never delays, blocks, or fails the
      commit; the strand for commit N lands shortly after N (and rides in N+1).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#pick"

  warpline-config:
    description: >-
      Per-repo engine toggles, `.warpline/config.json` (NATIVE-FIRST R1/R2;
      .paradigm/research/warpline-native-first/roadmap-native-first.md). OPT-IN
      switches only — defaults never change because a config exists. Keys:
      shadowGate (default false) — when true, every #pick also records the
      #shadow-gate observe-only verdict; stake (default: valve OFF) — the
      #stake checkpoint valve's S4 toggle {enabled, refs allowlist, branch,
      repo, author} plus the R2 auto-stake cadence stake.auto
      ('every-seal'|'daily'|false, default false — S4 still rules); and gate
      (R2, default agentWrites:'shadow') — gate.agentWrites:'real' makes an
      AGENT-ATTRIBUTED #pick ENFORCE its admit verdict (blocking on a
      would-not-seal verdict; humans keep the git door byte-identically). The
      leakage deny-list is deliberately NOT here (D5: constitution-grade
      frozen constant in #stake-guard, never a config flag). A missing config
      is the empty config; a MALFORMED config throws (a corrupt toggle file
      must not silently read as "everything off") — the seal path catches for
      humans (fail-safe) but REFUSES agent-attributed picks (fail-closed: a
      corrupt toggle must not silently disable the real gate); interactive
      callers surface it.
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first]

  stake:
    description: >-
      THE CHECKPOINT VALVE (Phase 1 native-first, T-2026-07-17-001, founder
      ask; arky-architecture.md §3 + aegis-security.md §3). `warpline stake`
      exports ONE sealed fabric state as ONE git commit on a DEDICATED stake
      branch (default warpline-stakes; working-branch names refused) — git's
      demoted role: a familiar, read-only checkpoint shelf. D3: stakes are a
      FIRST-PARENT LINEARIZATION (each stake's single parent = the previous
      stake commit; git receives checkpoints, never the weave topology).
      Safeguards: S1 committed `.warpline-stake` marker + trailer-only message
      (`warpline-stake` + PickId/StateId/TreeId/Schema, zero prose); S2
      allowlist-by-materialization — the tree is built by restoreTree from the
      bound object store into a clean temp dir, then the post-build audit walks
      the built bytes against the #stake-guard deny-list + untrusted-prose
      content markers (nothing touches the git odb until the audit passes); S3
      verify-or-refuse — built tree re-hashed from disk must equal BOTH
      binding.treeId and the shadow gitOid RECOMPUTED from the store (never
      read from s.binding.gitOid), and `rev-parse <commit>^{tree}` must equal
      the pure-TS expectation before the ref CAS-advances; S4 default OFF +
      per-ref allowlist + EVERY invocation (stake/skip/refuse/recover) appends
      a stakeAudit:v1 row to `.warpline/stakes/audit.jsonl`; S5 `warpline
      stake recover <commit>` verifies the reset worktree hashes to the staked
      strand's WORKTREE-SEMANTICS EXPECTATION (T-2026-07-18-005, the F3
      drill-#1 false-refusal fix: a worktree:v1 binding IS the expectation; a
      legacy-git binding's expectation is projectTreeWorktreeSemantics of the
      authenticated binding tree — each strand judged under ITS OWN recorded
      semantics) then MOVES refs/heads/selvage (or the legacy selvage) under
      the fabric lock — a ref move, NEVER an import or a new strand
      (post-recover edits parent on the staked pick via the normal seal path).
      The cut RECORDS worktreeTreeId (additive stakeAudit field + StakeResult)
      — the honest recovery expectation, on the audit record for any strand;
      recover records it too. Pinned by test/tree-semantics.test.ts (a
      legacy-semantics stake recovers GREEN after `git reset --hard`; an
      edited tree still refuses). Idempotent:
      re-staking the tip pickId SKIPS with an audit note. Gitlink-bearing
      trees refuse (bytes Warpline does not own). Commit objects are written
      as loose objects via #stake-git — no index, no filters, byte-exact.
      R2 AUTO-STAKE (T-2026-07-18-001, "the valve stakes every seal"):
      maybeAutoStakeOnSeal — config-gated cadence (stake.auto:
      'every-seal'|'daily'|false, default false) invoked best-effort by the
      #pick and #admit seal paths after a successful NON-SHADOW seal; S4 still
      rules (enabled + allowlist checked BEFORE invoking, so a disabled
      cadence never spams refuse rows); 'daily' skips while a stake/skip ran
      within 24h; a valve failure NEVER blocks the seal and every actual
      invocation audits itself; a byteOnly seal (#pick byte custody) counts —
      doc-only commits reach the valve. FIRST LIVE STAKE cut 2026-07-18: commit
      440fec31bd57e6cd61d87adbe06a22d08a63028f on warpline-stakes (the exact
      sealed state the v1 deny-list had refused); SECOND (post-tree-semantics-
      fix) 0f4b2ffe9d47, parent 440fec31 — F3 drill #2 recovered it GREEN
      (f3-drills.jsonl n=2); 440fec31 itself remains recoverable via the
      recorded-semantics projection (validated byte-for-byte against drill-1
      evidence).
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, valve, security]
    components:
      - "#stake-guard"
      - "#stake-git"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#restore"
      - "#warpline-config"
      - "#fabric-refs"

  stake-guard:
    description: >-
      The S1 one-way guards + the D5 CONSTITUTION-GRADE deny-list of the
      checkpoint valve — stake-denylist:v2 (R2, T-2026-07-18-001: the v1
      basename-global + byte-substring rules refused the FIRST REAL stake on
      four false positives — source/spec/tests QUOTING the envelope marker,
      and a paradigm-events verdicts.jsonl hit by basename). v2 is FROZEN +
      schema-versioned in FIVE constants with colocated pure matchers:
      STAKE_DENY_NAMES (any-depth structural/secret names — .git, .warpline
      wholesale [a stake is a reset target; fabric content inside one would
      stomp the live fabric — tracked .warpline files are fine IN GIT but
      never in a stake tree, which by construction the snapshot walk cannot
      produce anyway], .warpline-stake, daemon-tokens/session-keys.jsonl);
      STAKE_DENY_PATHS (ROOT-ANCHORED sidecar homes under .warpline/ — belts
      pinned by name so narrowing the wholesale rule can never silently expose
      a sidecar); STAKE_DENY_ENVELOPE_KINDS + STAKE_DENY_ROW_SCHEMAS +
      STAKE_DENY_ROW_SHAPES (SHAPE-AWARE content rules: only PARSED
      .json/.jsonl content matching an envelope object, a sidecar schema tag,
      or a sidecar field signature refuses — source/markdown/tests can never
      match). Any change is a SCHEMA CHANGE (bump the tag + re-pin the
      freeze-test digest in test/stake.test.ts), never a config flag.
      assertNotStakeInput is the S1 choke point wired into #absorb (covering
      #pick and the #hook auto-seal path): a worktree carrying the marker, a
      ref in the stake namespace, or a commit whose tree carries the marker is
      REFUSED as input; #backfill leaves marker-bearing provenance commits
      unbound. Cycle-free by design (imports only fs/config/git-exec) so
      absorb can import it. Regressions pinned in test/stake-denylist-v2.test.ts
      (each live false positive is a MUST-PASS fixture; true leaks still refuse).
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, valve, security]

  stake-git:
    description: >-
      The ONLY module in Warpline allowed to WRITE to a git repository, fenced
      to the checkpoint valve. Two mutations exactly: (1) loose blob/tree/
      commit objects constructed in pure TS (git's exact framing + sha1, zlib,
      write-tmp + atomic rename) — no `git add`, no index, no clean/smudge/
      autocrlf filters, so staked bytes can never be rewritten in flight; (2)
      the stake ref advanced via `git update-ref <ref> <new> <old>` per-ref
      CAS — never a checkout, never HEAD/index/worktree. sha256-object-format
      repos refuse fail-closed. buildCommitBody is first-parent-only BY SHAPE
      (a single optional parent — a stake commit structurally cannot be a
      merge; D3). #git-exec stays read-only by contract.
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, valve]
    components:
      - "#git-exec"

  shadow-gate:
    description: >-
      R1 of the native-first dogfood ladder (loid-loops.md §1) — the organic
      evidence clock. shadowAdmit runs the FULL #admit pipeline (claim gate,
      verdict, trust-floor escalation, coverage labels, knot-payload build) in
      OBSERVE-ONLY mode: never seals, never moves the selvage, never writes
      fabric/objects/sidecars — the ONLY write is one `shadowVerdict:v1` row
      appended to `.warpline/shadow/verdicts.jsonl` {ts, ref, agentId, status,
      confidence, knots, coverage, wouldSeal, durationMs, escalation?,
      claimReport?}. ROW BOUNDS (T-2026-07-17-007): the symbol arrays (knots/
      agentChanged/otherChanged) cap at SHADOW_ROW_CAP=50 sorted entries with
      ADDITIVE exact-total fields (knotsTotal/agentChangedTotal/
      otherChangedTotal) — a WORKTREE verdict over a 24k-symbol change is a
      ~5KB row; under-cap verdicts keep full symbol fidelity; schema stays v1
      (additive only, G1). Feeds organic-K3, HELD precision (would-have-held counts),
      and the F1a latency baseline BEFORE any cutover risk. Surfaces: `warpline
      admit --shadow`, and the #pick auto-seal path when #warpline-config sets
      shadowGate:true. Zero-mutation invariant pinned byte-for-byte in
      test/shadow-admit.test.ts. Rows are LOCAL telemetry (gitignored); shadow
      never pollutes the real claims/grades calibration streams — its judgments
      ride the row.
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, telemetry]
    components:
      - "#admit"
      - "#warpline-config"

  native-write-path:
    description: >-
      PHASE 0 of NATIVE-FIRST (arky-architecture.md §2.1 — THE KEYSTONE; ratified
      D2): the worktree→pick→admit loop with NO git anywhere, T-030's successor.
      fork mints the agent's scratch ref at the selvage PICKID (I9; optional
      restore into the agent worktree); **propose SEALS a v3 SCRATCH STRAND from
      the worktree** — snapshotDir (native walk, I5-INDEXED: the .warpline/index
      stat cache rehashes only changed files, so a warm propose walk on this
      monorepo costs ~1s not 7s) → absorbTree (restore from the
      store → lens, I2: sealed meaning provably corresponds to snapshotted
      bytes) → buildStrandV3 (bind-on-seal, parents=[scratch base], claim
      attached via sidecar, provenance git-null per I4) — advancing ONLY the
      scratch ref: proposals are durable, addressable, exchangeable BEFORE
      judgment; admit weaves scratch-tip × selvage through the EXISTING decision
      engine (admitDecision + claim gate + trust floor verbatim), materializes
      via materializeMergedStateNative ONLY (pure object-store 3-way; no git
      merge machinery on this path), seals the v3 weave (parents=[selvageTip,
      scratchTip], recipe folded into the pickId) with per-ref CAS on
      refs/heads/selvage, and on CLEAN RESTORES the merged bytes back into the
      agent worktree (§2.1 step 5 — the agent continues from merged reality; no
      commit ever existed). FAST_ADMIT is a selvage FAST-FORWARD to the scratch
      strand (a v3 admission is a ref advance, not a re-seal). KNOT/DANGLE
      persist the #knot-payload (both sides' treeIds native) and refuse — the
      scratch ref keeps the durable work; resolveNative seals the council
      resolution as a v3 weave carrying KnotResolution. EVERY refusing verdict
      (KNOT / DANGLE / CLAIM-BREACH / HELD, incl. the byte-conflict downgrade)
      carries the SAME #refusal object as the git-era gate, built by the shared
      exported builders with native-path ladders ({native:'true'}) —
      T-2026-07-21-007: this is the path agents actually use, and a refusing
      native verdict without the F4 carrier made cold-agent recovery impossible
      exactly where it matters most. PW-2 SEQUENCING REFUSALS (mcp-skin-spec
      §4, pinned site table): every prerequisite boundary throws RefusedError
      with a typed ladder instead of prose — legacy fabric → UNSUPPORTED/
      refs.migrate(human); legacy stateId scratch → UNSUPPORTED/retry-corrected/
      fork; admit-without-propose (THE cold mistake) → BAD_REQUEST/
      retry-corrected/[fork, propose]; disjoint DAG → INTEGRITY_BROKEN/escalate;
      resolve-no-selvage → NOT_FOUND/never; resolve-no-scratch → NOT_FOUND/
      retry-corrected/propose. PW-10 FORK CLOBBER GUARD: re-fork over a sealed,
      unadmitted proposal refuses (BAD_REQUEST/retry-corrected/admit ladder)
      via CAS #scratch writes — before this, same-principal concurrent sessions
      silently orphaned each other's proposals. Asserted site-by-site in
      test/native-sequencing-refusals.test.ts. Every step is an append
      or a CAS. SCOPE: v3 writes for scratch strands + native weaves only — the
      git-era seal path stays v2 until the founder-visible cutover (no selvage
      cutover here). EXIT PROOF: test/native-loop-no-git.test.ts — the full
      fork→propose→admit→KNOT→resolve→restore loop green in a directory with no
      `.git` at all (I12), pure-v3 DAG authenticated by fabric verify.
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, phase-0]
    components:
      - "#scratch"
      - "#admit"
      - "#refusal"
      - "#materialize"
      - "#seal"
      - "#fabric-refs"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#knot-payload"
      - "#claim"
      - "#restore"

  warplined:
    description: >-
      PHASE 1 of NATIVE-FIRST (arky-architecture.md §1.1; roadmap PHASE 1): the
      solo daemon — THE FABRIC WITH A NETWORK FACE. No database beside it: every
      verb calls the SAME engine function the CLI calls, in-process, under the
      SAME #fabric-lock (G4 — no other mutation path). Transport: NDJSON over a
      unix domain socket (.warpline/daemon.sock, mode 0600 — Aegis stage-1 trust
      boundary is the OS user; loopback TCP is a later opt-in flag). One JSON
      line = one frame; envelope {rpc:'warplined:v1', id, verb, token, params} →
      {id, ok, result|error}; `result` is the ENGINE SHAPE VERBATIM (G3:
      AdmitResult, knotPayload:v1, GradeReport, shadowVerdict:v1, StakeResult).
      ONE ERROR VOCABULARY (SP2, TD-2026-07-21-766): RpcErrorCode is COLLAPSED
      into #refusal's RefusalCode (the daemon's six codes were already a strict
      subset — a pure widening, collapsed BEFORE any MCP tool schema could
      freeze the duplicate), and every error frame additionally carries the
      refusal:v1 object itself (error.refusal, built at the single response
      boundary so no failure site can forget it) — a cold agent on this skin
      branches on code/gate/retriable enums, never message prose;
      DaemonRpcError passes it through typed. Verdict-class codes
      (GATE_REFUSED/CLAIM_BREACH/TRUST_HELD/STALE_BASE) arrive INSIDE engine
      results (AdmitResult.refusal), never as transport errors.
      MCP PRE-WORK (mcp-skin-spec §4): the boundary detects engine-thrown
      RefusedError and emits its carried refusal verbatim (PW-2); the AUTH
      refusal carries the mint escalation ladder (PW-3c: daemon.token.mint,
      principal human); `status` is STATE-AWARE (PW-6, additive — the RELOCATED
      F4 carrier since hosts truncate tool descriptions): cycle with stages,
      the caller's position (scratchPresent/scratchIsPickId/proposalSealed/
      behindSelvage), nextLegalVerbs, the verb→tool map, and the static
      untrusted-content sentence; `knot.show` takes summary:true (PW-7 —
      payload minus file bodies, summary marker honest); the audit row gains
      resultCode (PW-8 — a verdict-class refusal inside an ok result is audited,
      not masked; same derivation as exitCodeForResult; #f4-trace, not the
      audit, is F4 ground truth). Teaching text is single-sourced from
      #warplined-descriptors; MCP token custody (PW-9) = mint --mcp writes
      .warpline/daemon/mcp.token (0600) and mcpAgentToken() reads ONLY env
      WARPLINE_MCP_TOKEN then that file — never daemon-tokens.jsonl.
      Verbs (1:1 onto engine fns — transport + identity, never logic): status,
      refs.list, fork, propose, admit (native, incl. the R1 shadow flag),
      knot.show, resolve, stake, stake.recover, grade.report, shadow.tail,
      backup (#warpline-backup — snapshot to a dest dir, the one write that
      lands OUTSIDE .warpline/).
      STAGE-1 IDENTITY (aegis-security.md §1.2): every request carries a bearer
      token (#warplined-tokens); the daemon SERVER-STAMPS actor/agentId/decidedBy
      from the resolved principal — client-supplied identity params are advisory
      and ignored. The verb×principal matrix (§2.2): resolve / stake /
      stake.recover / backup and the accept-breach / accept-risk override flags
      are HUMAN-CLASS ONLY (an agent never accepts its own breach); engine-side
      override records (claim evaluations, grade escalations) are reused, not
      duplicated. READ-SCOPE CEILING (PHASE 1 close-out, console lane): a
      scope:'read' token (#warplined-tokens) is capped at READ_ONLY_VERBS
      (status, refs.list, knot.show, grade.report, shadow.tail) — checked
      structurally BEFORE dispatch, so even a human-class read token holds no
      write capability. Chosen over tokenless local-socket reads: stage 1 says
      no anonymous reads (sidecars are trust data), so the console stays
      tokened + stamped + audited, just least-privilege. EVERY call appends a daemonAudit:v1 row —
      .warpline/daemon/audit.jsonl {ts, principal, kind, verb, target, ok, code}
      including refusals; targets are STRUCTURAL (selectors/paths/flags), never
      prose (§4.1: log the address, never the body). LIFECYCLE: exactly one
      daemon per fabric — O_EXCL pidfile lock (.warpline/daemon.pid), live-pid
      probe refuses a second start, dead-pid/orphaned-socket residue recovered
      on start, SIGTERM stops clean (socket+pidfile unlinked). CLI: `warpline
      daemon start|stop|status|token mint|token list|call`. BYTE-IDENTITY PROOF
      (Loid R4, pulled early): test/daemon-byte-identity.test.ts — the same
      fixture loop (genesis FF, edit FF, CLEAN WEAVE) in-process vs through the
      socket produces byte-identical results modulo the envelope. NEVER started
      against this repo's live fabric by tests — fixtures only.
    type: service
    tags: [warpline, native-first, phase-1, daemon]
    components:
      - "#warplined-tokens"
      - "#warplined-client"
      - "#native-write-path"
      - "#refusal"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#shadow-gate"
      - "#knot-payload"
      - "#grade"
      - "#stake"
      - "#fabric-refs"

  warplined-tokens:
    description: >-
      Aegis stage-1 identity for #warplined: per-principal bearer tokens in
      .warpline/daemon-tokens.jsonl (append-only daemonToken:v1 rows
      {token, principal, kind:'human'|'agent', createdAt, scope?}; file mode
      0600; gitignored via .warpline/*; the filename is ALSO on the frozen stake
      deny-list — never leaves the box twice over; #warpline-backup excludes it
      from snapshots for the same reason). Minting is LOCAL-CLI-ONLY
      (`warpline daemon token mint <name> --kind human|agent [--scope read]`) —
      the human's act, gated by possession of the box; no self-service daemon
      verb exists (anti-sockpuppet, §2.2). SCOPE (additive, G1): absent = full
      surface for the kind; 'read' = the CONSOLE class, capped server-side at
      READ_ONLY_VERBS; an UNKNOWN scope fails closed (the row is skipped, never
      resolved full-power). consoleReadToken() is the platform router's
      discovery hook — it surfaces ONLY the newest console+read row, so the
      console structurally never holds a full-power token. resolveToken does
      constant-time comparison and fails CLOSED (garbled rows never match);
      `token list` is redacted (tokens are shown exactly once, at mint). Honest
      trust claim: attribution is authentic within this machine's OS-user
      boundary; the daemon is the trusted stamper — per-strand signatures
      remain M3.
      MCP TOKEN CUSTODY (PW-9, mcp-skin-spec D2/R5): `token mint <name> --kind
      agent --mcp` additionally writes the bare token to
      .warpline/daemon/mcp.token (0600; --mcp refuses non-agent kinds — an
      ambient MCP process must never hold human capability); mcpAgentToken()
      mirrors the consoleReadToken structural pattern — env WARPLINE_MCP_TOKEN
      first, else the dedicated file, NEVER daemon-tokens.jsonl (it holds human
      tokens; one "newest row" bug there is a privilege escalation). No
      revocation ceremony at stage 1 — rotation = mint anew (documented in the
      mint help).
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, phase-1, security]

  warplined-descriptors:
    description: >-
      The CANONICAL verb descriptors (PW-5, mcp-skin-spec §4; Loid requirement
      3): ONE source of teaching text per daemon verb — {verb, summary (1-2
      sentence HARD budget), paramsSchema, cycleStage
      (orient|fork|propose|admit|inspect|resolve|custody), principal
      (agent|human)} — consumed by the MCP tool registration and #warplined's
      state-aware status, so skins cannot drift into parallel vocabularies (G3
      applied to teaching text). toolNameOf() is the ONE naming law between
      dotted daemon verbs and MCP tool names (knot.show → warpline_knot_show);
      wire shapes and refusal.next[].verb keep the dotted names verbatim.
      agentSurfaceVerbs() derives the default MCP surface = every
      principal:'agent' descriptor (human verbs OMITTED per Aegis R2). The
      WHOLE table is CONTENT-ADDRESSED (descriptorsId, sha256 of canonical
      serialization) — the FG-3 pre-registration anchor stamped into every
      #f4-trace row: T-005 pins it before the first scored F4 batch, ANY change
      resets the ≥10-run denominator, and test/descriptors-frozen.test.ts is
      the tripwire that makes a change impossible to miss. Carries the static
      untrusted-content sentence for the prose-carrying tools (admit /
      knot.show / shadow.tail).
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, F4, mcp]
    components:
      - "#warplined"

  f4-trace:
    description: >-
      f4Trace:v1 — the skin's OWN measurement stream and the F4 harness's
      GROUND TRUTH (mcp-skin-spec §5; Loid requirement 2 — daemonAudit:v1 was
      REJECTED for the job: audit(true) masks verdict-class refusals riding
      inside ok results, rows carry no runId, and the wasted-turn classifier
      needs the prior refusal's enums). One row per tool/CLI call appended to
      .warpline/f4/trace.jsonl: {schemaVersion, ts, runId ($WARPLINE_F4_RUN_ID,
      'unscored' outside a harness run — rows always emit), seq (per-process
      ordinal), skin:'mcp'|'cli', principal, verb (dotted daemon name), target
      (structural only — selectors/flags, NEVER prose), ok, refusal? (the
      refusal:v1 VERBATIM, captured from error frames AND result.refusal),
      resultClass? (structural: sealed | decision.status | noop | read),
      descriptorsId (which teaching text served the call — FG-3)}. F4Tracer is
      the per-process handle (fixes identity, counts seq; append is
      BEST-EFFORT — a trace failure never fails the serving call);
      resultClassOf derives the class from result SHAPE, never text. The W1-W4
      wasted-turn taxonomy is the HARNESS's pure classifier over these rows —
      this module only emits faithfully.
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, F4, measurement]
    components:
      - "#refusal"
      - "#warplined-descriptors"

  warplined-client:
    description: >-
      The thin typed client of #warplined (DaemonClient): NDJSON frames over the
      unix socket, id-correlated (concurrent calls on one connection answer in
      order), engine shapes deserialized VERBATIM — the client never reshapes
      (G3). DaemonRpcError carries the machine-readable refusal code
      (AUTH/FORBIDDEN/BAD_REQUEST/UNKNOWN_VERB/NOT_FOUND/ENGINE);
      daemonAvailable() is the socket-presence auto-detect hook. This is the
      seam the platform router / judgment console re-points to in the console
      lane (not this lane). In-process engine calls remain the CLI default;
      `warpline daemon call <verb>` is the explicit transport path (token from
      --token or WARPLINE_DAEMON_TOKEN).
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, phase-1]
    components:
      - "#warplined"

  warpline-backup:
    description: >-
      PHASE 1 close-out — the custodianship tax's first payment: `warpline
      backup <dest>` (fabric/backup.ts; also the daemon's HUMAN-CLASS `backup`
      verb). ATOMIC snapshot of the whole fabric into `<dest>/.warpline/**`:
      the mutable core (fabric.jsonl ledger, refs, sidecars, stake+daemon
      audits) copies while HOLDING #fabric-lock (no writer can interleave a
      half-appended ledger), then the object store copies lock-free
      (content-addressed, immutable once written, no gc — anything the staged
      ledger references already existed); everything stages in a sibling temp
      dir on dest's own volume and ONE rename publishes the backup (a crash
      never leaves a half-backup). COPY = fs.copyFile COPYFILE_FICLONE — a CoW
      clone on APFS, a full byte copy elsewhere; NEVER hardlinks (a hardlink
      shares the inode, so an in-place ledger append or source tamper would
      mutate the "backup"). EXCLUDED, each named: daemon-tokens.jsonl (SECRETS
      — D5 never-leaves-the-box), daemon.sock/daemon.pid (live process
      residue), refs/.lock* (process state); daemon/audit.jsonl INCLUDED
      (accountability survives a dead disk; structural targets, never prose).
      MANIFEST `<dest>/backup.manifest.json` (warplineBackup:v1): per-file
      bytes + sha256 digested from the STAGED bytes, plus counts
      {files, objects, ledgerRows, refs}; lives BESIDE .warpline so the
      backup's fabric dir holds only fabric. `warpline backup verify <dest>`:
      recompute every digest (missing/extra/size/digest problems flagged —
      an ADDITION is tamper evidence too) + run the FULL #fabric-verify
      authentication against the copy; ok ⇔ zero problems AND zero fabric
      failures; CLI exit 1 on any. THE RESTORE PATH IS THE ENGINE ITSELF:
      a backup IS a home-fabric root — every read AND write verb works
      against it directly (proved in test/backup.test.ts: open → refs
      identical → verify green → a NEW strand proposes/admits against the
      backup → still green). Refuses an existing dest and a dest inside the
      source fabric.
    type: module
    tags: [warpline, native-first, phase-1, custodianship]
    components:
      - "#fabric"
      - "#fabric-lock"
      - "#fabric-refs"
      - "#fabric-verify"
      - "#warplined"

  oracle:
    description: >-
      oracle(branchA, branchB) — merge-base → absorb base/A/B → diff → predict →
      git merge-tree reality (read-only) → score the confusion matrix
      (agreeClean/agreeConflict/divergeGitOnly/divergeMeaningOnly/score/verdict) →
      append an OracleRecord to .warpline/oracle.jsonl. The two divergence cells are
      the experimental result. Conflict paths map to symbols at .purpose-dir level;
      a git conflict in a NON-SYMBOL file (or an unresolvable merge) becomes
      gitConflictUnmapped — a git-only divergence that gates the verdict, so the
      oracle NEVER reads CONVERGENT over a real git conflict (T-2026-06-25-001). The
      ACTIONABLE answer is a separate mergeClean flag (no knots ∧ no dangling ∧ no git
      conflict); convergence measures meaning⟷git AGREEMENT, so a contested merge both
      sides predict scores 'convergent agreement' but is NOT mergeClean — read
      mergeClean for "is it safe to weave?" (T-2026-06-25-005). KNOT-SIZE RANKING
      (T-2026-07-03-002, additive fields — verdict/score untouched, so the committed
      base-rate evidence stays valid): convergence also partitions divergeMeaningOnly
      into directContested[] (own content changed on ≥1 side) vs rippleOnly[]
      (flagged only via essence transitivity) + knotSize (=|directContested|, the
      ranking/threshold key) and flagCount. Ground truth: ≤6-symbol flag sets were
      50% churn-validated, every ≥10-symbol avalanche 0% — small knots ARE the
      product; the CLI names direct symbols and collapses ripple to a count line.
      P3 GAP-1 (G1-additive, schemaVersion unchanged): the record also carries
      coverage — the #honesty per-path labels over every path either side touched
      (real refs only) + aggregate counts. A coverage failure never fails the
      oracle.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#predict"
      - "#justification"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#git-exec"
      - "#honesty"

  weave:
    description: >-
      The pre-merge MEANING forecast + the semantic-diff report — READ-ONLY and,
      unlike #oracle, never writes .warpline/. forecast(A, B) reuses the Oracle
      pipeline up through predict() (mergeBase → absorb base/A/B → diff → predict)
      and surfaces it as pre-merge UX: a CLEAN-vs-DECISIONS verdict, autoClean
      count, knots (symbol + contending sides), dangling (from→target, retiredBy).
      An optional vsGit flag also runs the full Oracle (git merge-tree) read-only
      for the meaning-vs-bytes divergence comparison. semanticDiff(refA, refB)
      groups the SemDeltas (born / retired / contract-changed-with-slots /
      renamed-noop) so the "rename/move is the EMPTY delta" property is visible on
      real refs. The `weave` WRITE verb is RESERVED, not implemented in Phase 1.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#predict"
      - "#oracle"
      - "#git-exec"

  consolidate:
    description: >-
      The N-way fold FORECAST (read-only) — the N-branch generalization of #weave's
      forecast(). consolidate(refs[], {base?}) absorbs a common base + N refs IN
      PARALLEL (concurrency-safe via git-archive materialization, T-2026-06-23-003),
      diffs each against base, and runs the proven 2-way #predict PAIRWISE across
      every (i<j), aggregating by symbol: an N-way knot is the union of the pairwise
      knots on a stableKey (sides = the refs in tension), a dangle is an edge one ref
      added into a symbol another ref retired, autoFolded = touched keys with no
      decision. decisions (K_human) = knots + dangles. NO git merge, NO ledger write.
      The engine behind the falsifiable multi-agent claim (T-2026-06-24-012): a
      meaning-preserving edit (reorder, local rename) carries ZERO delta and
      auto-folds where git textually conflicts, so human labor tracks GENUINE
      meaning-collisions, not branch count. Engine only — no CLI verb wired yet.
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#sem-delta"
      - "#predict"
      - "#git-exec"

  lifeline:
    description: >-
      Meaning-aware blame — lifeline(symbol, {maxCommits}) traces a symbol's ESSENCE
      through the commits that touched its file (git log --follow, so the thread
      survives the renames/moves git blame resets on), attributing each essence-change
      to the commit that INTRODUCED it (newest-first) with the author + INTENT (the
      commit subject git blame can't show). Resolves the symbol at HEAD, absorbs up to
      maxCommits of the file history IN PARALLEL (concurrency-safe per T-2026-06-23-003),
      and emits ONLY genuine meaning changes — a commit that touched the file but not
      the symbol's meaning is NOT an event. Read-only. MVP: tracks by the ::qualified
      suffix (rename-stable across file moves; a same-suffix collision elsewhere is a
      known limitation).
    type: module
    components:
      - "#absorb"
      - "#git-exec"

  warpline-cli:
    description: >-
      The warpline command line (commander). `warpline oracle <a> <b> [--json]`,
      `warpline absorb <ref> [--json]`, `warpline weave --preview <a> <b> [--vs-git]
      [--json]` (the pre-merge forecast; the bare `weave` write verb is reserved
      and refuses with a read-only notice), `warpline consolidate <refs...> [--base R]
      [--json]` (the N-way fold forecast — CLEAN-to-fold vs N decisions, knots with
      per-ref sides, dangling, auto-fold count), and `warpline diff [refA] [refB]
      [--json]` (semantic diff; no args = WORKTREE vs HEAD, one arg = ref vs HEAD,
      two args = refA vs refB). Plus `warpline status [--json]` (the working tree's
      MEANING vs HEAD — the named, discoverable form of diff-no-args) and `warpline
      lifeline <symbol> [--max N] [--json]` (meaning-aware blame — who changed a
      symbol's meaning, when, why; survives renames). Phase-2 WRITE PATH: `warpline
      pick [-m <intent>] [--as <actor>] [--confidence <n>] [--ref <ref>] [--quiet]
      [--json]` seals meaning into the fabric (no-op when unchanged; with `--ref
      <commit>` and no -m, intent + actor are derived from the commit), `warpline
      log [--max N] [--json]` shows this project's native meaning-history (the
      strands, newest first, with the git coexistence anchor), `warpline selvage
      [--json]` shows the current fabric tip + the strand that sealed it, and
      `warpline hook <install|uninstall|status>` manages the post-commit auto-seal,
      `warpline scratch <agentId>` forks a per-agent multi-writer base, `warpline
      admit <agentId> [--ref <ref>] [--json]` runs the admission protocol (FAST_ADMIT
      / CLEAN+confidence / KNOT / DANGLE) — its exit code is VERDICT-KEYED off
      the result's #refusal via exitCodeForResult on both the git-era and
      --native paths (0 sealed/NOOP, 1 GATE_REFUSED, 3 CLAIM_BREACH,
      4 TRUST_HELD, 5 STALE_BASE — T-2026-07-21-006: a refusing verdict must
      never exit 0; --shadow stays exit 0 by contract, observe-only), and
      `warpline resolve <agentId> --ref
      <resolvedRef> -m <reason> [--by <who>] [--ours <ref>]` seals a KNOT-council
      resolution, and `warpline grade [--window N] [--dry] [--json]` grades strand
      confidence against real survive/overturn outcome (the moat), and `warpline
      fabric verify [--json]` authenticates the whole PICK-DAG (integrity + v2 chain
      + binding re-derivation; exit 0 intact / 1 tamper / 2 I/O), and `warpline
      restore [selector] [--to <dir>] [--force] [--json]` reconstructs a working
      tree from the native object store with git ABSENT (the layer→VCS threshold —
      selector HEAD|selvage|N|@N|pick:|state:|tree:, path-hardened, A4-refuses an
      unbound strand, dirty-dest guarded), and `warpline refs
      <migrate|list> [--json]` (V3.2 pickId refs: `migrate` is the ONE-TIME
      founder-visible stateId→pickId selvage conversion; `list` shows
      refs/heads/* + the head set — legacy repos show the single ledger tip).
      `fabric verify` additionally reports the v3 DAG segment + abandoned heads.
      The only module allowed to write stdout; library code stays quiet.
    type: cli
    components:
      - "#oracle"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#weave"
      - "#consolidate"
      - "#lifeline"
      - "#pick"
      - "#fabric"
      - "#fabric-verify"
      - "#fabric-refs"
      - "#restore"
      - "#hook"
      - "#scratch"
      - "#admit"
      - "#refusal"
      - "#resolve"
      - "#grade"

flows:
  oracle-run:
    description: >-
      The end-to-end Oracle flow: merge-base, absorb base/A/B, diff each side,
      synthesize justifications, predict the merge from meaning, run git's real
      merge read-only, score the confusion matrix, append the OracleRecord.
    steps:
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "Resolve merge-base(A, B)"
      - component: "#absorb"
        action: "Lift base, A, B to WarpStates (detached temp worktrees)"
      - component: "#sem-delta"
        action: "diff(base, A) and diff(base, B)"
      - component: "#justification"
        action: "Synthesize justA / justB from git log + ripple"
      - component: "#predict"
        action: "predict(ΔA, ΔB) → autoClean / knots / dangling"
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "git merge-tree --write-tree A B (read-only) for the real conflict set"
      - component: "#oracle"
        action: "Score the confusion matrix and append the OracleRecord to .warpline/oracle.jsonl"

  absorb-flow:
    description: >-
      One ref -> a WarpState of MEANING (stage 3a). Materialize a read-only ref
      tree (git archive | tar, no worktree), live-parse it, lift+inject TS/TSX
      code-units as synthetic nodes in the SAME universe, then compute whole-state
      essences and stamp every object. The lens only reads the tree — the Phase-1
      read-only invariant holds.
    steps:
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "git archive | tar -x materialize the ref into a temp dir (no worktree, lock-free, read-only)"
      - component: "#lift-code-units"
        action: "liftCodeUnits(worktree) then injectCodeUnits(index, units) — synthetic code-unit nodes spliced in BEFORE essence computation"
      - component: "#essence-hash"
        action: "computeEssences over the merged .purpose+code universe (code-units take the <algo>:ts<exact> tag, e.g. v1.1:ts5.9.3; .purpose stays v0)"
      - component: "#warp-state"
        action: "buildWarpState stamps each object's contentId and derives the stateId; remove the worktree in a finally"

  pick-flow:
    description: >-
      The single-writer WRITE PATH (Phase 2) — seal the working MEANING into this
      project's OWN durable history. Absorb the working tree, diff against the
      parent selvage (the source of truth for change), no-op on an empty diff, else
      seal a #strand, persist the snapshot, append to the #fabric ledger, and
      advance the selvage atomically. Writes .warpline/ only — git is read for
      provenance (HEAD sha, user.name) and never mutated.
    steps:
      - component: "#absorb"
        action: "Lift the working tree (or a ref) to a WarpState of MEANING"
      - component: "#fabric"
        action: "readSelvage — the parent tip (null at genesis)"
      - component: "#sem-delta"
        action: "diff(parent, current) — empty ⇒ no-op; else summarize born/retired/contract-changed/renamed"
      - component: "#warp-store"
        action: "putState — durably persist the snapshot under .warpline/states/"
      - component: "#pick"
        action: "Seal the #strand (attribution + reserved confidence + git-HEAD anchor); appendStrand; writeSelvage (atomic publish)"

  weave-forecast:
    description: >-
      The pre-merge MEANING forecast (read-only, ephemeral — no ledger write).
      Reuses the Oracle pipeline up through predict() only; git is consulted just
      when --vs-git is requested.
    steps:
      - component: "#git-exec"
        action: "Resolve merge-base(A, B)"
      - component: "#absorb"
        action: "Lift base, A, B to WarpStates (detached temp worktrees)"
      - component: "#sem-delta"
        action: "diff(base, A) and diff(base, B)"
      - component: "#predict"
        action: "predict(ΔA, ΔB) → autoClean / knots / dangling → CLEAN vs DECISIONS verdict"
      - component: "#weave"
        action: "Optionally (--vs-git) run the full #oracle read-only for the meaning-vs-bytes divergence"

aspects:
  ~read-only:
    description: >-
      THE GIT-SAFETY INVARIANT (Phase-1 read-only; PRESERVED by the Phase-2 write
      path) — Warpline NEVER mutates the user's git HEAD/index/worktree. A ref is
      read by materializing its tree via `git archive` into a throwaway temp dir (no
      worktree, no .git/worktrees lock); the cold git<2.38 merge-tree fallback uses a
      detached worktree torn down in a finally. The ONLY disk Warpline writes is
      under .warpline/ — the content-addressed store, the oracle.jsonl ledger, AND
      the Phase-2 native history (#fabric: refs/selvage + fabric.jsonl + states/).
      The write path is a write to Warpline's OWN store, NOT to git: git is read for
      provenance (HEAD sha, user.name) only. Lifting a ref, or the WORKTREE
      pseudo-ref, only READS files — the code-lens included.
    category: invariant
    severity: critical
    tags: [safety, read-only, git, coexistence]
    anchors:
      - src/git/git-exec.ts:1-16
      - src/git/git-exec.ts:128-180
      - src/absorb.ts:37-66
      - src/git/repo-lock.ts:1-22
      - src/warp/store.ts:53-64
      - src/fabric/fabric.ts:1-20
      - src/fabric/pick.ts:1-26
    applies-to:
      - "#git-exec"
      - "#repo-lock"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#consolidate"
      - "#lifeline"
      - "#lift-code-units"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#warp-store"
      - "#oracle"
      - "#weave"
      - "#fabric"
      - "#pick"

  ~determinism:
    description: >-
      THE THESIS — absorbing the same ref twice is byte-identical (stateId + every
      per-symbol contentId + repo-relative filePath), so diff(absorb(HEAD),
      absorb(HEAD)) is provably ZERO and two machines on the same source agree. No
      timestamps/paths/positions/prose/embeddings enter the hashed byte stream;
      every set is sorted+deduped; strict canonical JSON; sorted file enumeration;
      deterministic synthetic code-unit ids; the TS compiler is exact-pinned and
      stamped, WITH the CCNF algorithm version, into the code essence tag
      (essence:<algo>:ts<exact>, e.g. essence:v1.1:ts5.9.3) so a different
      compiler OR serialization algorithm is a different content-address
      namespace, never a silent collision.
    category: invariant
    severity: critical
    tags: [thesis, determinism, hashing, reproducibility]
    anchors:
      - src/warp/warp-state.ts:106-111
      - src/lens/ts-essence.ts:374-393
      - src/absorb.ts:1-14
      - test/absorb.test.ts:54-77
    applies-to:
      - "#essence-hash"
      - "#canonical-json"
      - "#code-essence"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#warp-state"
      - "#absorb"
      - "#consolidate"

  ~meaning-coverage:
    description: >-
      THE TD-806 HONESTY METRIC — Warpline reports WHERE it has meaning versus
      where it degrades to opaque bytes (the 3-tier fidelity model). A code-unit the
      TS lens cannot soundly lift (unresolved refs, unsupported forms) is flagged
      reducedFidelity — never silently empty; coverage is surfaced, not assumed.
      Honest about its own blind spots so the meaning layer's thickness is legible.
    category: rule
    severity: medium
    tags: [honesty, fidelity-tiers, td-806, coverage]
    anchors:
      - src/lens/ts-lens.ts:391-431
      - src/lens/lift-code-units.ts:120-135
    applies-to:
      - "#lift-code-units"
      - "#ts-lens"
      - "#code-lens"
